var e=[{id:`case-apple-pay`,pathId:`case-studies`,order:0,title:`Apple Pay — 41M txn/day`,subtitle:`Apple Pay's most interesting security property: your real card number is never on your phone. A merchant gets a one-time-use cryptogram tied to a device-specific token. Even if eve`,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[],story:{headline:`The dump that came back dead.`,paragraphs:[`It's 3 AM on the fraud desk of a mid-size issuing bank, about a year after Apple Pay launched. A dump from a breached merchant chain just surfaced on the dark web — 4.7 million 'cards.' The analysts run them through the authorisation simulator the way they always do, sorting live from dead. Half the list dies instantly.`,`The dead half is Apple Pay. Not because the tokens were detected and blocked — because they were never secrets to begin with. The attackers are holding Device Account Numbers and single-use cryptograms that were already spent at the till. On the carding forums, the threads fill with the same bitter word: duds. Meanwhile, every live card in the dump turns out to be a magstripe clone or a plain old PAN.`,`That's the night it clicks for the team: thirty years of building thicker walls around the card number, and the winning move was making sure the number never existed where attackers could reach it. The PAN went from the most stolen string on the internet to not worth the disk space it takes to store.`],problem:`How do you let a phone authorise real money — 41 million times a day — without the phone, the merchant, or Apple ever holding the one secret the entire fraud economy is built around?`},tech:{stack:[`Secure Element`,`Device Account Number`,`One-time Cryptogram`,`EMV Contactless`,`NFC`,`TSP De-tokenisation`],note:`The Secure Element holds a per-device token (the DAN) and a signing key; every tap mints a one-time EMV cryptogram that the card network routes to the issuer's TSP for de-tokenisation and authorisation.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why not just encrypt the card number?`,body:[`So you've got 41 million taps a day and one question: where does the secret live? The obvious first move is encrypt the PAN on the phone and decrypt it somewhere downstream. It feels safe — until you ask who holds the decryption key. Whoever that is becomes the juiciest target on the planet, and you haven't removed the vault, you've just moved it.`,`Tokenisation flips the problem. The phone never receives the PAN at all — it gets a Device Account Number that routes like a card but opens nothing. The DAN-to-PAN mapping lives with the issuer's TSP, behind the network, behind the bank. A breach of the phone, the merchant, or even Apple yields numbers that authorise nothing.`,`And there's a bonus the encryption design can't give you: revocability. Each DAN is per-device, so losing your phone kills one token while the plastic in your pocket keeps working. Try doing that with an encrypted PAN.`],quote:`Encryption hides a secret. Tokenisation deletes it.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Encrypt PAN on device`,`Works on legacy rails`,`Key and ciphertext co-located; one breach = every number`,`Poor`],[`Tokenise (DAN)`,`Leak reveals nothing; revocable per device`,`Needs TSP + network token support`,`Excellent`],[`Static PAN (magstripe)`,`Universally accepted`,`Cloneable, replayable, skimmable`,`Legacy only`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Apple Pay — 41M txn/day`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-apple-pay.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what actually lives in the Secure Element`,body:[`Inside the Secure Element: the DAN, a device-specific private key, and a monotonically increasing counter. That's the whole kingdom. The SE is a separate chip — the OS, your apps, and Apple's own code can read exactly none of it. Biometrics are just the doorman; Face ID doesn't sign anything, it authorises the SE to sign.`,`Why not software keys, host card emulation style? Cheaper, easier to provision over the air — but now your key material lives in the OS kernel's neighbourhood, one kernel bug away from extraction. Hardware isolation is the only boundary that survives a fully compromised operating system.`,`The counter is the quiet hero. Every cryptogram increments it, so a captured cryptogram is a spent bullet — replay it and the counter check kills it at the issuer before a cent moves.`],quote:`The most secure key is the one the operating system can't even see.`,table:{headers:[`Property`,`Secure Element (eSE)`,`Host card emulation (HCE)`],rows:[[`Key extraction`,`Tamper-resistant chip; OS can't read`,`One kernel bug away`],[`Provisioning`,`Slower, issuer-managed`,`Instant, over-the-air`],[`OS compromise`,`Token survives`,`Key likely gone`],[`Cost per device`,`Extra silicon`,`Software only`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Apple Pay — 41M txn/day`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-apple-pay.html`}},{heading:`03 · The three-second relay: what happens when you tap`,body:[`Tap. The SE mints a cryptogram — DAN, amount, counter, timestamp — signed with the device key, and beams it over NFC. Here's the elegant part: the terminal doesn't know it's Apple Pay. It sees an EMV contactless transaction, same as a chip card. No merchant integration, no new protocol, decades of rails reused.`,`The network spots the tokenised PAN range and routes to the issuer's TSP, which swaps DAN for the real PAN and asks the bank for an authorisation. Approval flows back down the same path. Apple is nowhere in this loop — no Apple server touches the transaction. Provisioning is Apple's job; payment is the banks'.`,`The whole relay — sign, tap, de-tokenise, authorise, respond — has to land inside the contactless terminal's timeout. About three seconds, end to end, at 41 million transactions a day. That's the real engineering constraint here: correctness at street-crossing speed.`],quote:`Apple built the on-ramp, then stayed out of the payment's way.`,table:{headers:[`Actor`,`Sees`,`Never sees`],rows:[[`Secure Element`,`DAN, device key, counter`,`Real PAN`],[`Merchant terminal`,`One-time cryptogram`,`PAN, keys, anything Apple`],[`Card network`,`Tokenised PAN`,`Real PAN (until TSP)`],[`Issuer TSP`,`Token-to-PAN mapping`,`Device key`],[`Apple`,`Provisioning metadata`,`The transaction itself`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Apple Pay — 41M txn/day`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-apple-pay.html`}},{heading:`04 · The endgame: making the PAN not worth stealing`,body:[`Step back and look at the fraud economics. For thirty years the PAN was the crown jewel — skimmers, dumps, carding forums, all monetising one static number. Apple Pay's move wasn't better defence of the number; it was making the number absent. A breached merchant's stolen-data dump now contains tokens that are dead on arrival.`,`Per-device keys cap the blast radius: compromise one phone, burn one token. Remote wipe kills the DAN instantly, and because provisioning runs through the issuer, a bank can revoke a token without reissuing plastic. The attacker's cost-benefit quietly inverted.`,`That's the lesson I keep coming back to for any system with a valuable secret: don't build a bigger vault around it — architect the system so the secret isn't there to steal.`],quote:`Don't build a bigger vault — make the jewel worthless.`,table:{headers:[`What the attacker steals`,`Street value`,`Why`],rows:[[`Magstripe PAN`,`High — cloneable`,`Static number, no signature`],[`Chip PAN`,`Moderate`,`Signed, but the number is still static`],[`Apple Pay DAN + cryptogram`,`Zero`,`Single-use, counter-bound, no PAN behind it`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Apple Pay — 41M txn/day`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-apple-pay.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`provisioning`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Provisioning: the PAN stops at the issuer`,caption:`Adding a card to Wallet — the real PAN never lands on the phone; only the DAN and a device key reach the Secure Element.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    W["Wallet app: add card"] --> A["Apple TSP"]
    A --> B["Issuing bank: verify card"]
    B --> D["DAN issued + device key"]
    D --> SE["Secure Element: DAN + key loaded"]
    B -. "real PAN never follows" .- SE`},{id:`tap-flow`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Tap to pay: the three-second relay`,caption:`One-time cryptogram over NFC, routed like a chip transaction, de-tokenised at the issuer's TSP — Apple is absent from the entire loop.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant SE as Phone — Secure Element
    participant T as Merchant terminal
    participant N as Card network
    participant TSP as Issuer TSP
    participant I as Issuing bank
    SE->>T: Tap — cryptogram (DAN, amount, counter, time) via NFC
    T->>N: EMV contactless authorisation
    N->>TSP: Tokenised PAN — route to de-tokenisation
    TSP->>I: Real PAN + auth request
    I-->>TSP: Approve / decline
    TSP-->>N: Response
    N-->>T: Response
    T-->>SE: Approved — ~3s end to end`}],takeaways:[`Tokenise, don't encrypt: a leaked encrypted PAN is a breach, a leaked DAN is noise — remove the secret instead of hiding it.`,`Bind every signature to a counter: one cryptogram per transaction means captured traffic replays to a dead end.`,`Keep keys in silicon: if the OS can read the key, assume a kernel bug eventually will — hardware isolation is the only boundary that holds.`,`Budget the whole relay at ~3 seconds: sign, tap, de-tokenise, authorise must fit inside the contactless timeout at 41M txn/day.`,`Per-device credentials cap blast radius: one compromised phone burns one token — never the card, never the fleet.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Apple Pay — 41M txn/day`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-apple-pay.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`case-bitly`,pathId:`case-studies`,order:1,title:`Bitly — URL shortener`,subtitle:`"Design a URL shortener." Asked in every coding interview for a decade. The toy version fits in 30 lines. The real Bitly handles ~10 billion clicks a month, half of which are bots.`,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[],story:{headline:`The night half our traffic wasn't human.`,paragraphs:[`It's 9:47 PM on a Friday when a pop star tweets a bit.ly link to a merch drop. Within minutes the dashboard that normally peaks around 4k clicks/sec is doing a hundred times that. The redirect path doesn't even flinch — cache hit, 301, microseconds. This is the part we spent years making boring, and boring is exactly what saved us.`,`The thing on fire isn't the redirect. It's the truth. Kafka buffers the burst fine, but the analytics pipeline is hours behind, and when it finally catches up, the customer's dashboard proudly reports 40M clicks. Their conversion rate has cratered. Because somewhere around half of those 'clicks' were bots — scrapers, prefetchers, link crawlers — and we hadn't filtered them before they hit the pipeline.`,`Monday's retro produces one sentence on the whiteboard: the redirect is the product; everything else is a side effect. Every design decision since — batch-minted keys, near-infinite cache TTLs, fire-and-forget analytics, bot filtering at the edge — falls out of that sentence.`],problem:`Map short code → long URL and return a 301 in microseconds at ~10B clicks/month. Absorb 100× viral bursts. Capture click analytics without ever touching the hot path. Serve custom branded domains with per-customer SSL.`},tech:{stack:[`Base-62 Codes`,`Key Generation Service`,`MySQL`,`Redis / Memcached`,`Kafka`,`Cassandra`,`301 Redirects`,`Host-Header Routing`],note:`A deliberately dumb redirect hot path — edge bot filter, cache, one indexed MySQL read — fed by a batch-minting key service, with click events streamed through Kafka to async analytics and custom domains resolved via the Host header.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why the 30-line version lies to you`,body:[`Everyone's written the toy: hash the URL, take 7 characters, insert, lookup, 301. And every real system hits the same wall — hashes collide, so you retry, and now your write path has a loop in it. Random codes with retry-on-collision work, but they don't scale linearly: as the table fills, every insert probes more.`,`Base-62 of an auto-increment ID is the elegant answer — 62^7 ≈ 3.5 trillion codes from 7 characters — until someone notices that /aB3xK9 and /aB3xKa are neighbors. Sequential codes are enumerable; a competitor can crawl your entire link inventory. So you either encrypt the sequence or decouple codes from IDs entirely.`,`What actually works at scale is boring: a key generation service that pre-mints codes and hands them out in blocks. No coordination on the hot path, no collision retries, no enumeration. The interview question hides all of this — the constraints aren't in the prompt, they're in the numbers you have to ask for.`],quote:`The constraints aren't in the prompt — they're in the numbers nobody says out loud.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Hash of URL`,`Deterministic, stateless`,`Collisions force a retry loop`,`Toy only`],[`Random + retry`,`Simple, non-enumerable`,`Probe cost grows as table fills`,`Small scale`],[`Base-62 of auto-ID`,`Zero collisions, tiny codes`,`Sequential, so enumerable`,`Needs encryption layer`],[`Key-gen service`,`No hot-path coordination`,`One more service to run`,`Production`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Bitly — URL shortener`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-bitly.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the read path: cache hard, redirect dumb`,body:[`Ten billion clicks a month, and nearly every single one is the same operation: this code maps to that URL. That's a cache designer's dream — a read-heavy workload where the values almost never change. So Redis or Memcached sits in front of MySQL with a hit rate north of 95%, and the TTL is essentially infinite because URLs rarely change after creation.`,`Invalidation only matters on the rare edit, and a rare edit can afford a cache purge. The redirect itself is a 301 — permanent — which means browsers cache it too and never come back to ask. That's free load-shedding, but it's also lost analytics: a 301 the browser remembers is a click you never see. Which redirect code you pick is a product decision wearing a technical costume.`,`The discipline is keeping this path dumb. No personalization, no per-request logic beyond the lookup. The moment someone wants to 'just check one thing' inline, you've turned a microsecond redirect into a microservice call chain.`],quote:`The redirect path should be so boring it's invisible.`,table:{headers:[`Redirect`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`301 permanent`,`Browsers cache it; huge load shed`,`Repeat clicks invisible to analytics`,`High-volume links`],[`302 temporary`,`Every click hits you; full analytics`,`All traffic stays on your fleet`,`Analytics-critical products`],[`307 / 308`,`Preserves method on re-POST`,`Rarely what a shortener needs`,`API link shortening`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Bitly — URL shortener`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-bitly.html`}},{heading:`03 · Minting keys: never negotiate uniqueness on a request path`,body:[`Here's the pattern that makes the whole thing scale linearly: a central key service mints about a million codes at a time, and each web server grabs a batch of a thousand and burns through them locally. When the batch runs dry, ask for another. No distributed counter on the hot path, no race conditions, no check-then-insert retries.`,`The obvious objection: what if a server dies holding 900 unused codes? You've 'lost' them. With 3.5 trillion codes in the keyspace, losing a thousand is a rounding error you can't even measure. This is the trade people get wrong in interviews — they optimize for not wasting codes and end up coordinating on every single write.`,`Batch size is the only real knob. Too small and you're chatty with the minting service; too big and crashes waste keyspace. A thousand is the sweet spot for a fleet of web servers — seconds of local supply, trivial waste.`],quote:`Pre-pay for uniqueness in batches — never auction it off per request.`,table:{headers:[`Batch size`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`~10 codes`,`Near-zero waste`,`Constant minting round trips`,`Tiny fleets`],[`~1,000 codes`,`Seconds of local supply, low waste`,`Crash loses a trivial batch`,`Web-server fleet`],[`~1M codes`,`Minting service barely notices you`,`Big waste on crash`,`The minting service itself`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Bitly — URL shortener`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-bitly.html`}},{heading:`04 · Custom domains: one service, many names`,body:[`Enterprise customers don't want bit.ly/abc on their marketing emails — they want brand.co/abc. The trick is that the redirect handler doesn't actually care which hostname it's serving: it reads the Host header, resolves which customer namespace that domain belongs to, and does the exact same lookup it always did.`,`One service, many domains. The operational cost lives at the edge, not in the code: SSL means a cert per customer or a wildcard, and DNS means customers point a CNAME at you. The lookup itself barely changes — the domain is just another dimension of the key.`],quote:`The brand lives in the Host header; the system underneath doesn't change.`,source:{label:`sharpoverride — Bitly — URL shortener`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-bitly.html`}},{heading:`05 · Analytics and bots: fire-and-forget, filter early`,body:[`Every redirect is a click event — who, when, geo from IP, referrer, user-agent. At 10B clicks a month that's ~4k events per second on average, and a viral spike does a hundred times that. If analytics shares a thread with the redirect, your product becomes your logging. So the redirect path fires the event at Kafka and returns the 301 in microseconds; the pipeline consumes asynchronously and aggregates into a wide-column store (Cassandra, historically).`,`Best-effort is the contract: if Kafka backs up during a burst, redirects don't notice. And here's the uncomfortable number — up to half of Bitly's traffic is bots. Filter early: per-IP rate limits, user-agent classification, CAPTCHA on suspicious patterns. Otherwise the burst passes, the pipeline catches up, and your customer's dashboard reports 40M 'engagements' that were mostly machines.`],quote:`If analytics shares a thread with the redirect, your product becomes your logging.`,table:{headers:[`Bot defense`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Per-IP rate limit`,`Cheap, runs at the edge`,`NATs and proxies get caught`,`First line`],[`UA classification`,`Catches known crawlers`,`Bots spoof user agents`,`Always on`],[`CAPTCHA challenge`,`Stops sophisticated abuse`,`Adds friction for real humans`,`Suspicious patterns only`],[`Post-hoc filtering`,`Zero user friction`,`Dashboards already lied`,`Reporting repair`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Bitly — URL shortener`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-bitly.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`bitly-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Redirect hot path vs. async analytics`,caption:`The hot path is three hops (edge, cache, DB); everything expensive — key minting and click analytics — lives off to the side.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  U["Browser / bot traffic"] --> E["Edge: rate limit + UA filter"]
  E --> R["Redirect service"]
  H["Host header routing"] --> R
  R -- "lookup code" --> C[("Redis / Memcached")]
  C -- "miss" --> M[("MySQL: code to long_url")]
  K["Key generation service"] -- "batches of 1000" --> W["Write API"]
  W --> M
  R -- "301 redirect" --> U
  R -- "click event" --> Q[["Kafka"]]
  Q --> P["Analytics pipeline"]
  P --> S[("Click store")]`},{id:`redirect-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One redirect, end to end`,caption:`The 301 returns before the click event is even durably queued — analytics is best-effort by design.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  participant U as Browser
  participant E as Edge
  participant R as Redirect
  participant C as Cache
  participant D as MySQL
  participant K as Kafka
  U->>E: GET brand.co/abc123
  E->>R: forward with Host header
  R->>C: lookup abc123
  alt cache hit, over 95% of requests
    C-->>R: long_url
  else cache miss
    R->>D: SELECT long_url WHERE code = abc123
    D-->>R: long_url
    R->>C: warm cache, near-infinite TTL
  end
  R-->>U: 301 Location: long_url
  R--)K: click event, fire-and-forget
  Note over K: analytics pipeline aggregates async`}],takeaways:[`Size the keyspace first: 7 base-62 chars = 62^7 ≈ 3.5 trillion codes — enough for billions of links with headroom to burn.`,`Keep the hot path to two lookups: cache (>95% hit rate, near-infinite TTL) in front of one indexed MySQL read, then a microsecond 301.`,`Mint keys in batches of ~1,000 from a central service — zero coordination or collision retries on the request path, and a crashed server wastes a rounding error of keyspace.`,`Never let analytics share the redirect's thread: fire-and-forget to Kafka; at ~4k events/sec average with 100× viral bursts, async is the only architecture that survives.`,`Filter bots at the edge — up to half of clicks aren't human, and unfiltered numbers turn every customer dashboard into a lie.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Bitly — URL shortener`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-bitly.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`},{kind:`youtube`,label:`Hello Interview — Beginner System Design Interview: Design Bitly w/ a Ex-Meta Staff Engineer`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iUU4O1sWtJA`,author:`Hello Interview`,note:`Beginner system design: Bitly.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`case-cloudflare-postgres`,pathId:`case-studies`,order:2,title:`Cloudflare — 55M RPS, 15 Postgres clusters`,subtitle:`Cloudflare runs its core configuration store on a small number of large Postgres clusters. Not Spanner. Not Cassandra. Postgres — operated extremely well. The story is what "extrem`,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[`NVMe`,`Postgres`,`partitioning`,`pgbouncer`],story:{headline:`The WAF rule that landed in Postgres in 900 milliseconds — and at the edge, never.`,paragraphs:[`2:47 AM, Frankfurt. A credential-stuffing campaign is walking through one enterprise customer's login endpoint, and the on-call engineer does the thing on-call engineers do: pushes an emergency WAF rule from the dashboard. The write commits to the security-policies cluster in under a second. CPU barely twitches. And then the edge keeps serving the attack. The rule is in the database. It is not on the edge. Somewhere between those two facts, the night gets very long.`,`First hypothesis: the database is down. It isn't — 140 terabytes of NVMe, idling. Second hypothesis: replication lag. Also no. The actual culprit is two hops away from the headline number: a partition-maintenance job holding an ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock on the config table, plus a newly deployed service whose pods had started opening direct connections — no pgbouncer — quietly eating max_connections. Postgres wasn't drowning in queries. It was drowning in connections and one badly scheduled lock.`,`The postmortem produced the sentence this entire lesson hangs on: the database behind 55 million requests per second serves a few thousand queries per second — and it can still take your edge down. 'Small QPS' and 'cannot be down' are not the same sentence. Everything Cloudflare does with those 15 clusters — one workload per cluster, partitioning discipline, pgbouncer everywhere, replicas for lag-tolerant reads, NVMe bare metal with the hot set in RAM — is what 'extremely well' turns out to mean.`],problem:`How do you run a control-plane database that sees ~0.01% of your traffic yet cannot tolerate a minute of downtime — and what does operating Postgres 'extremely well' actually require?`},tech:{stack:[`Postgres`,`pgbouncer`,`Native Partitioning`,`Read Replicas`,`NVMe Bare Metal`,`Edge Config Cache`],note:`Postgres clusters on NVMe bare metal are the source of truth for everything the edge does; pgbouncer fronts every connection, native partitioning keeps big tables manageable, and replicas feed lag-tolerant config fan-out. The 55M RPS is answered by edge caches — Postgres never sees that traffic.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: does 55M RPS actually hit Postgres?`,body:[`Let's defuse the scary number first, because it's doing all the work in the headline. 55 million requests per second is what Cloudflare's edge serves — HTTP requests hitting CDN PoPs around the globe. Almost none of those touch Postgres. The edge answers from cached configuration: your DNS records, your WAF rules, your page rules, fetched once and held right next to the request.`,`So what does Postgres actually see? Thousands of queries per second. Config refreshes, dashboard edits, API writes, billing updates. That's it. But here's the inversion that makes this architecture interesting: the least-trafficked database in the building is the one that cannot go down. A config change has to land at the edge in seconds. An enterprise customer pushing an emergency WAF rule mid-attack is not waiting for your replica to catch up.`],quote:`The number that scares you and the number that pages you are almost never the same number.`,table:{headers:[`Plane`,`Volume`,`Latency budget`,`If it fails`],rows:[[`Edge data plane`,`55M RPS`,`Sub-ms, served from cache`,`Degrades, keeps serving last-known config`],[`Control plane (Postgres)`,`Thousands of QPS`,`Seconds to propagate`,`Edge flies blind — no new config, no writes land`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Cloudflare — 55M RPS, 15 Postgres clusters`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-cloudflare-postgres.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: one cluster per workload`,body:[`Fifteen clusters. My first reaction seeing that number was 'why not one?' Here's the logic: one cluster per logical workload. DNS records live in one, security policies in another, billing in a third, customer accounts in a fourth. Each around 140 TB, each dedicated to a single concern.`,`The benefit is blast radius. A runaway migration on the billing cluster degrades billing. It does not blind the WAF. The cost is honest and it's operational: fifteen things to monitor, patch, upgrade, and fail over. You're buying isolation with toil — and Cloudflare decided that trade was worth it.`,`And notice what this is not: it's not per-tenant sharding. It's coarse, workload-level isolation — the kind you can still reason about at 3 AM.`],quote:`Fifteen clusters isn't fifteen times the work. It's fifteen times smaller the blast radius.`,table:{headers:[`Topology`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`One mega-cluster`,`One thing to operate`,`Every failure is global`,`Small platforms, few concerns`],[`One cluster per workload`,`Blast-radius isolation, independent scaling`,`15× patching, monitoring, failover drills`,`Cloudflare's choice — many distinct concerns`],[`Shard per tenant`,`Unbounded tenant growth`,`Cross-shard queries, hot tenants, rebalancing`,`Only when one workload outgrows one box`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Cloudflare — 55M RPS, 15 Postgres clusters`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-cloudflare-postgres.html`}},{heading:`03 · Partitioning before sharding — and the lock that stops the world`,body:[`Inside each cluster, the scaling tool of choice is partitioning, not sharding. Postgres native partitioning — split a big table by time or by tenant, and the database routes the rows for you. Same connection, same transactions, same planner. Compare that to sharding, where every cross-shard join becomes a distributed-systems problem (ask Figma).`,`But partitioning has a sharp edge, and it's the one from the opening story: partition maintenance — creating tomorrow's partition, detaching last month's — takes an ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock. Run that job at peak against a table your config readers hit, and you've built a stop-the-world pause into your control plane. Partition aggressively, and schedule the maintenance like a deploy. Because it is one.`],quote:`Sharding is a distributed-systems decision. Partitioning is a DBA decision. Always take the cheaper one first.`,table:{headers:[`Technique`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Native partitioning`,`Same DB, same transactions, planner routes rows`,`Maintenance locks; plan the partition lifecycle`,`Default for big tables`],[`Sharding`,`Scales writes past one node`,`Cross-shard joins, rebalancing, 2PC pain`,`When partitioning stops sufficing`],[`Read replicas`,`Cheap read scale, isolates dashboards`,`Replication lag; stale reads`,`Everything that tolerates lag`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Cloudflare — 55M RPS, 15 Postgres clusters`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-cloudflare-postgres.html`}},{heading:`04 · The connection math nobody does until 3 AM`,body:[`Now the boring-looking part of the stack that pages you first: pgbouncer. Here's the math nobody runs. Every service keeps a pool of, say, 20 connections. You have 40 pods. That's 800 connections aimed at one primary whose max_connections might be 300. Postgres doesn't run out of CPU first. It runs out of connections.`,`pgbouncer sits in front and multiplexes thousands of client connections onto a few dozen real ones, in transaction pooling mode. The rule at this scale is simple: nothing touches Postgres directly. Every path — dashboard, API, background worker — goes through the pool. And read traffic that tolerates lag goes to replicas, keeping the primary for writes.`],quote:`Your database doesn't die of load. It dies of connections.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`Behavior`,`Risk`],rows:[[`Direct connections per pod`,`Simple, no extra hop`,`Pods × pool size explodes past max_connections`],[`pgbouncer, session mode`,`Connection pinned per client session`,`Still scales with idle sessions`],[`pgbouncer, transaction mode`,`Connection assigned per transaction`,`Session state (prepared statements) needs care`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Cloudflare — 55M RPS, 15 Postgres clusters`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-cloudflare-postgres.html`}},{heading:`05 · Why not Spanner — and the hardware truth`,body:[`Last block, the question everyone asks: why not Spanner? Why not Cassandra, DynamoDB, MongoDB? Cloudflare considered them and stayed with Postgres. The reason is the most transferable lesson of the session: the database that scales is the one your team knows how to operate. Fifteen clusters of well-understood Postgres beat one exotic distributed database nobody can debug at 3 AM.`,`And the hardware story is refreshingly old-school. These clusters run on dedicated bare metal with NVMe SSDs — not RDS, not cloud VMs. The sizing rule is the same one from 2015: get the hot working set into RAM and keep the index hot. 140 TB per cluster sounds enormous until you realize the hot set — the config actually being read — is a sliver of that.`],quote:`Boring technology, operated extremely well, beats exciting technology operated adequately.`,table:{headers:[`Option`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Postgres on bare metal NVMe`,`Team mastery, full transactions, predictable perf`,`You own failover, patching, capacity`,`Cloudflare's choice`],[`Spanner-style NewSQL`,`Global consistency, horizontal writes`,`Operational novelty, cost, added latency`,`Genuinely global write-heavy state`],[`Cassandra / DynamoDB`,`Massive write throughput, tunable`,`No joins, eventual consistency, query limits`,`Data plane at extreme scale`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Cloudflare — 55M RPS, 15 Postgres clusters`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-cloudflare-postgres.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Control plane vs data plane`,caption:`The edge serves 55M RPS from caches; Postgres serves the config pipeline at thousands of QPS — one of 15 clusters shown.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  subgraph EDGE["Edge data plane · 55M RPS"]
    REQ["Customer request"] --> CACHE["Config cache per PoP"]
  end
  subgraph CTRL["Control plane · thousands of QPS"]
    DASH["Dashboard / API"] --> PB["pgbouncer"]
    PB --> PRI["Postgres primary<br/>one of 15 clusters<br/>~140 TB on NVMe"]
    PRI --> REPL["Read replicas"]
  end
  CACHE -- "config refresh, lag-tolerant" --> REPL
  DASH -- "writes" --> PB`},{id:`config-push`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Emergency WAF rule push`,caption:`Write path through pgbouncer to the primary, then config fan-out to the edge via replicas — the path that must complete in seconds.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant C as Customer dashboard
  participant API as Control API
  participant PB as pgbouncer
  participant PG as Postgres primary
  participant R as Read replica
  participant E as Edge PoP cache
  C->>API: Push emergency WAF rule
  API->>PB: INSERT rule, COMMIT
  PB->>PG: Run on pooled connection
  PG-->>PB: Write committed
  PB-->>API: Ack
  E->>R: Poll config version
  R-->>E: New rule set
  E->>E: Hot-reload WAF rules
  Note over C,E: Attack blocked at the edge within seconds — Postgres never saw a request`}],takeaways:[`Split the planes: if the edge caches config, size Postgres for thousands of QPS — refresh rate plus writes — never for the 55M RPS headline.`,`One cluster per logical workload: 15 clusters means a bad migration degrades one feature, not the platform — buy blast-radius isolation even at the cost of 15× operational toil.`,`Partition before you shard: native partitioning keeps one connection, one transaction, one planner — and schedule partition maintenance like a deploy, because its ACCESS EXCLUSIVE locks stop the world.`,`Nothing touches Postgres without pgbouncer: compute pods × pool size against max_connections before deploy day — Postgres runs out of connections before it runs out of CPU.`,`Keep the hot set in RAM on NVMe bare metal: 140 TB per cluster is fine when the working set is a sliver — the 2015 trick of a hot index still beats a distributed database you can't operate.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Cloudflare — 55M RPS, 15 Postgres clusters`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-cloudflare-postgres.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`case-discord-cassandra`,pathId:`case-studies`,order:3,title:`Discord — Trillion-message database`,subtitle:`Discord rewrote its message database twice. MongoDB cracked at 100M messages in 2015. Cassandra cracked at one trillion messages in 2022. ScyllaDB — the C++ rewrite of Cassandra — `,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[`MongoDB → Cassandra → ScyllaDB`],story:{headline:`The 3 a.m. page: one channel, 10,000× the writes`,paragraphs:[`It's launch night for a major game. A streamer's Discord goes from quiet to 10,000× its normal write rate in minutes. In the Cassandra ring, one node owns that channel's partitions — by design, because channel-scoped, time-ordered reads want sequential disk access. That node's CPU saturates, queries time out, the replicas behind it start to lag, and the cluster's tail latency degrades for everyone on the platform. Then a 200-millisecond JVM garbage-collection pause lands on top of the burst, queues pile up in cascade, and the on-call engineer is staring at dashboards that all say the same thing: this isn't a bug. This is the shape of the system.`,`The team had been here before. In 2015, MongoDB's message index — the B-tree mapping (channel_id, _id) to document location — outgrew RAM at ~100 million messages, and p99 reads climbed from 5 ms to 300 ms. Different database, same feeling: the system's shape had stopped matching the workload's shape. By 2022 Discord was writing 4 billion messages a day against a trillion-message corpus, and the honest summary was: 'Cassandra was always the problem we were managing, not the problem we were using.'`,`So they migrated. Again. Six months with a small team to leave MongoDB in 2015; a full year with a dedicated team to leave Cassandra for ScyllaDB — the C++ rewrite that kept the data model and dropped the JVM. The uncomfortable part is that both migrations were cheap relative to what they cut short: years of operational pain that would have arrived either way. 'We didn't pick the database. We picked the next database we could survive.'`],problem:`How do you hold a 1 ms p99 on a corpus that grows 10,000× while the working set stays pinned to the last hour of global activity — and every few years the database itself becomes the outage?`},tech:{stack:[`ScyllaDB`,`Cassandra`,`Rust Data Services`,`NVMe Direct I/O`,`Shard-Per-Core`,`Time-Bucketed Partitions`],note:`The moving parts this lesson touches, in the order the story meets them.

\`\`\`mermaid
timeline
    title Two migrations, one trillion messages
    2015 : MongoDB cracks at 100M messages (index outgrew RAM)
    2017 : Cassandra live, 1B messages per month
    2022 : 4B messages per day, trillion cumulative (hot partitions, GC, compaction)
    Now : ScyllaDB, same data model on a C++ runtime
\`\`\``},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: the access pattern is the architecture`,body:[`Before we argue about databases, look at what chat actually does. Most messages are read once, within seconds, by users who happen to be online. A small fraction get read repeatedly — pinned messages, channel scrollback, search hits. A vanishing fraction are read after a year. So the working set is the last ~hour of activity across the planet; everything older is cold storage that occasionally gets paged in. That's a time-shaped workload, and it should drive every decision downstream — the partition key, the cache, the compaction strategy, all of it.`,`Now the numbers that make it brutal. By 2017 Discord was inserting 1 billion messages per month; by 2022, 4 billion per day against a trillion-message corpus. Storage cost scales linearly with that — fine, disk is cheap. Read latency does not get to scale: a 1 ms p99 in 2015 has to still be a 1 ms p99 in 2025, on a corpus 10,000× bigger. And before someone in the room asks 'why not just cache the hot stuff?' — because 'hot' here is defined by time, not popularity. The cache would have to be the last hour of global activity, which is the working set, which is the database. Caching doesn't shrink this problem; it renames it.`],quote:`The hard part of the database isn't the writes. It's that the writes don't stop and the reads never get easier.`,source:{label:`sharpoverride — Discord — Trillion-message database`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-discord-cassandra.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the 2015 boundary: why MongoDB cracked at 100 million`,body:[`The 2015 design fits on a napkin, and that was a feature, not a red flag. One MongoDB replica set. One document per message, indexed on {channel_id, _id} for the query everyone actually runs — 'give me the last N messages in this channel before timestamp T.' Reads scale by load-balancing across secondaries; writes funnel to the primary. Small team, simple schema, done. If traffic had stayed at 2014 levels, this is still a fine design.`,`At ~100 million messages, the message index — the B-tree that maps (channel_id, _id) to document location — stopped fitting in RAM. Every query that wasn't perfectly hot started walking the index off disk, and p99 read latency climbed from 5 ms to 300 ms. Notice what kind of failure this is: not a query problem, a memory-shape problem. The instinct is 'add RAM,' but the working set grows with user activity — it's unbounded by construction. The MongoDB-native fix is sharding, but the access pattern is channel-scoped and time-ordered, and that didn't fit MongoDB's sharding model cleanly. So: six months, a small team, and a move to Cassandra.`],quote:`When p99 grows with corpus size, it's not a query problem — it's a memory-shape problem, and no index tuning buys back RAM.`,table:{headers:[`Option`,`What you get`,`What it costs`],rows:[[`Add RAM / bigger box`,`Buys months, not years — the working set grows with activity`,`Unbounded cost curve, same cliff ahead`],[`Shard in MongoDB`,`Native horizontal scale`,`Channel-scoped, time-ordered access doesn't fit the sharding model cleanly`],[`Migrate to Cassandra`,`Partition shape matches the query shape`,`6 months, small team, a new operational surface to learn`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Discord — Trillion-message database`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-discord-cassandra.html`}},{heading:`03 · The Cassandra years: right shape, wrong runtime`,body:[`Credit where due: Cassandra genuinely solved 2015's problem. Partition by (channel_id, time_bucket) — each large channel's history lives on a specific node's disk, and reads are sequential within a partition. That's the textbook-good design for chat at scale; the partition key is literally the query. If you're designing a message store today, start here.`,`The cracks came from three directions at once. Hot partitions: a viral channel — a Twitch streamer's Discord, a major game release, a leak — hits 10,000× the normal write rate, one node saturates its CPU, queries time out, replicas behind that node lag, and the cluster's tail latency degrades for everyone. Java GC compounds it: a 200 ms pause during a burst causes cascading queue buildups. And compaction: Cassandra merges SSTables to reclaim space and keep reads fast, but Discord's write rate outran compaction on the hot nodes — pending compactions backed up, disk usage ballooned, and reads slowed because they had to consult more SSTables. Compaction tuning became a team responsibility. A team that didn't want to be Cassandra DBAs was becoming Cassandra DBAs.`,`Here's the failure chain in one picture — three separate mechanisms converging on the same hot node:

\`\`\`mermaid
flowchart LR
  V["Viral channel: 10,000x write spike"] --> N["Hot node owns the partition"]
  N -->|CPU saturated| T["Query timeouts"]
  T --> L["Replica lag"]
  L --> TL["Tail latency for the whole cluster"]
  G["200ms JVM GC pause"] --> Q["Cascading queue buildup"]
  W["Writes outrun compaction"] --> S["SSTable backlog: disk balloons, reads slow"]
\`\`\``],quote:`Cassandra was always the problem we were managing, not the problem we were using.`,source:{label:`sharpoverride — Discord — Trillion-message database`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-discord-cassandra.html`}},{heading:`04 · Why not just tune it? Picking the next database we could survive`,body:[`The obvious counter-argument: Cassandra is mature, the data model is right — just tune it. We've all taken that deal, and sometimes it's correct. But look at what tuning buys you here. GC tuning shaves the pauses; it doesn't remove the JVM. Compaction strategy tweaks buy headroom; hot partitions are architectural — the partition key that makes cold reads sequential is the same key that routes a viral spike onto exactly one node. You cannot tune your way out of a shape mismatch.`,`So the real menu has three items: keep Cassandra and accept a permanent operational tax; shard hot channels at the application layer and own that complexity forever; or change the runtime and keep the model. ScyllaDB is the C++ rewrite of Cassandra — same data model, same protocol, shard-per-core, no JVM GC pauses. That last point is the entire economics of migration 2: a year, a dedicated team, but a protocol swap rather than a re-model. You're not re-architecting the system; you're re-implementing the same architecture without the garbage collector.`],quote:`We didn't pick the database. We picked the next database we could survive.`,table:{headers:[`Option`,`What you get`,`What it costs`],rows:[[`Keep tuning Cassandra`,`No migration; the team already knows it`,`Permanent DBA tax; hot partitions are architectural, GC pauses remain`],[`App-layer sharding of hot channels`,`Bursts spread across nodes`,`Forever-complexity in the app; rebuilding what the database should own`],[`ScyllaDB (C++ rewrite)`,`Same data model and protocol; shard-per-core; no JVM GC`,`A year, a dedicated team, a new runtime to learn`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Discord — Trillion-message database`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-discord-cassandra.html`}},{heading:`05 · The pattern behind both migrations`,body:[`Step back and both cracks are the same crack. MongoDB failed when the index's shape stopped matching the working set's shape — a B-tree that had to live in RAM against a working set defined by time. Cassandra failed when the partition's shape stopped matching the burst's shape — one node per channel against channels that go viral, on a runtime whose pauses are measured in hundreds of milliseconds against a 1 ms p99 budget. Databases don't fail at capacity; they fail at shape mismatch.`,`And the economics, because that's the part everyone gets backwards. Migration 1: six months, a small team. Migration 2: a year, a dedicated team. Both were cheaper than the years of operational pain they avoided — pain that would have arrived either way. The skill isn't picking the perfect database; it's noticing the shape mismatch early enough that the migration is a project instead of an incident.`],quote:`Databases don't fail at capacity. They fail when their shape stops matching your workload's shape.`,table:{headers:[`Migration`,`Trigger`,`Cost`],rows:[[`2015: MongoDB → Cassandra`,`Index outgrew RAM at ~100M messages; p99 went 5 ms → 300 ms`,`6 months, small team`],[`2022: Cassandra → ScyllaDB`,`Hot partitions, GC pauses, compaction backlog at ~1T messages`,`A year, dedicated team`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Discord — Trillion-message database`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-discord-cassandra.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`case-discord-cassandra-fig1`,kind:`svg`,title:`Architecture before`,caption:`Fig. AThe 2015 architecture was textbook: one replica set, document model. Discord ran it for two years before scale forced a redesign.`,svg:`<svg viewBox="0 0 1200 320" class="diagram-svg" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet">
            <defs>
              <marker id="dca" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" fill="#c2410c"/></marker>
            </defs>
            <text x="20" y="30" font-family="JetBrains Mono" font-size="13" letter-spacing="0.14em" fill="#c2410c">FIG. A · 2015 — ONE MONGO REPLICA SET</text>
            <line x1="20" y1="40" x2="1180" y2="40" stroke="#1a1814" stroke-width="1"/>

            <g transform="translate(60, 80)">
              <rect x="0"   y="0"  width="160" height="50" fill="#1a1814"/>
              <text x="80"  y="30" font-family="JetBrains Mono" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f1ece1">CHAT API</text>

              <rect x="240" y="0"  width="220" height="50" fill="#c2410c"/>
              <text x="350" y="30" font-family="JetBrains Mono" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f1ece1">MONGODB PRIMARY</text>

              <rect x="240" y="80" width="220" height="50" fill="#1a1814"/>
              <text x="350" y="110" font-family="JetBrains Mono" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f1ece1">MONGO SECONDARY</text>

              <rect x="240" y="160" width="220" height="50" fill="#1a1814"/>
              <text x="350" y="190" font-family="JetBrains Mono" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f1ece1">MONGO SECONDARY</text>

              <line x1="160" y1="25" x2="238" y2="25" stroke="#c2410c" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#dca)"/>
              <line x1="460" y1="25" x2="460" y2="80" stroke="#c2410c" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/>
              <line x1="460" y1="105" x2="460" y2="160" stroke="#c2410c" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/>

              <text x="540" y="22" font-family="IBM Plex Sans" font-size="12" fill="#1a1814">Single replica set.</text>
              <text x="540" y="40" font-family="IBM Plex Sans" font-size="12" fill="#1a1814">All writes to primary.</text>
              <text x="540" y="58" font-family="IBM Plex Sans" font-size="12" fill="#1a1814">Reads scaled by hitting secondaries.</text>
              <text x="540" y="82" font-family="IBM Plex Sans" font-size="12" fill="#c2410c">Worked until 100M messages.</text>
              <text x="540" y="100" font-family="IBM Plex Sans" font-size="12" fill="#c2410c">Then the index stopped fitting in RAM.</text>
            </g>
          </svg>`},{id:`case-discord-cassandra-fig2`,kind:`svg`,title:`Architecture after`,caption:`Fig. BTwo layers: a Rust request-coalescing service in front, a shard-per-core C++ database underneath. Discord rebuilt both.`,svg:`<svg viewBox="0 0 1200 380" class="diagram-svg" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet">
            <defs>
              <marker id="dcb" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" fill="#c2410c"/></marker>
            </defs>
            <text x="20" y="30" font-family="JetBrains Mono" font-size="13" letter-spacing="0.14em" fill="#c2410c">FIG. B · 2023 — DATA SERVICES + SCYLLA</text>
            <line x1="20" y1="40" x2="1180" y2="40" stroke="#1a1814" stroke-width="1"/>

            <g transform="translate(40, 80)">
              <rect x="0"   y="0"  width="160" height="50" fill="#1a1814"/>
              <text x="80"  y="30" font-family="JetBrains Mono" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f1ece1">CHAT API</text>

              <rect x="240" y="0"  width="220" height="50" fill="#c2410c"/>
              <text x="350" y="22" font-family="JetBrains Mono" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f1ece1">DATA SERVICES (Rust)</text>
              <text x="350" y="40" font-family="JetBrains Mono" font-size="8" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f1ece1">request coalescing · cache</text>

              <rect x="500" y="0"   width="200" height="50" fill="#c2410c"/>
              <text x="600" y="30" font-family="JetBrains Mono" font-size="11" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f1ece1">SCYLLA CLUSTER</text>

              <rect x="740" y="0"   width="160" height="50" fill="#1a1814"/>
              <text x="820" y="22" font-family="JetBrains Mono" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f1ece1">NVMe per node</text>
              <text x="820" y="40" font-family="JetBrains Mono" font-size="8" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f1ece1" opacity="0.7">shard-per-core</text>

              <line x1="160" y1="25" x2="238" y2="25" stroke="#c2410c" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#dcb)"/>
              <line x1="460" y1="25" x2="498" y2="25" stroke="#c2410c" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#dcb)"/>
              <line x1="700" y1="25" x2="738" y2="25" stroke="#c2410c" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/>
            </g>

            <g transform="translate(40, 180)">
              <text x="0"  y="14" font-family="Instrument Serif" font-style="italic" font-size="16" fill="#1a1814">Two architectural moves, not one.</text>

              <text x="0"  y="46" font-family="JetBrains Mono" font-size="11" fill="#c2410c">Data Services</text>
              <text x="180" y="46" font-family="IBM Plex Sans" font-size="12" fill="#1a1814">Rust service that fronts every DB read.</text>
              <text x="180" y="64" font-family="IBM Plex Sans" font-size="12" fill="#1a1814">Coalesces duplicate concurrent reads.</text>
              <text x="180" y="82" font-family="IBM Plex Sans" font-size="12" fill="#1a1814">Same hot key requested 1k times = 1 backend read.</text>

              <text x="0"  y="114" font-family="JetBrains Mono" font-size="11" fill="#c2410c">ScyllaDB</text>
              <text x="180" y="114" font-family="IBM Plex Sans" font-size="12" fill="#1a1814">Cassandra API · C++ shard-per-core architecture.</text>
              <text x="180" y="132" font-family="IBM Plex Sans" font-size="12" fill="#1a1814">No JVM GC pauses · 1/3 the nodes for the same load.</text>
              <text x="180" y="150" font-family="IBM Plex Sans" font-size="12" fill="#1a1814">Compaction throughput keeps up with write rate.</text>
            </g>
          </svg>`}],takeaways:[`The hard part of the database isn't the writes. It's that the writes don't stop and the reads never get easier.`,`The database that scales is the one your team can operate. Everything else is choice paralysis.`,`Design the partition key around your hottest query — then stress it at 10,000×. Hot partitions are architectural, not tunable.`,`If p99 grows with corpus size, it's a memory-shape problem. No index tuning fixes an index that doesn't fit in RAM.`,`Same data model, new runtime is the cheapest migration you'll ever do — ScyllaDB kept Cassandra's model and dropped the JVM. Budget the migration before the outage schedules it for you.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Discord — Trillion-message database`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-discord-cassandra.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`case-dropbox`,pathId:`case-studies`,order:4,title:`Dropbox — File sync`,subtitle:`Dropbox shipped a magical product: a folder that "just stays in sync" between devices. Under the hood: chunked block storage, content-addressable hashing, delta sync, conflict reso`,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[`AWS`,`RAG`],story:{headline:`Moving an exabyte while the folder keeps syncing`,paragraphs:[`It's 2016, deep into the Magic Pocket migration. Tonight, a slice of production block traffic flips from S3 to racks Dropbox owns outright. The rule is unforgiving: a user in Berlin edits a spreadsheet at 3 AM and the folder must not stutter — no missing block, no stale copy, no 'syncing…' spinner that never ends.`,`The reason it's survivable at all is the protocol itself. Every block is immutable and named by its SHA-256, so the question 'do you have block X?' has the same answer whether X lives on S3 or on Dropbox's own SMR disks. The team copies the universe in the background, hash by hash, and flips lookups only when a block verifiably exists in both places. Content-addressing turns a terrifying cutover into a reconciliation problem.`,`By the end, ~90% of stored bytes live on Magic Pocket — erasure-coded 10+4, replicated across regions — and the AWS bill drops by roughly $75M a year. The folder just keeps syncing. Nobody outside the company ever knows there was a night.`],problem:`How do you sync files across devices with minimal bandwidth — and then move an exabyte of blocks off AWS without the folder ever noticing?`},tech:{stack:[`4 MB Blocks`,`SHA-256 Hashing`,`Content-Defined Chunking`,`Rabin Fingerprint`,`Delta Sync`,`Long-Poll Notify`,`Metadata DB`,`Magic Pocket`,`Erasure Coding 10+4`,`SMR Disks`,`AWS S3`],note:`Client-side chunking, hashing, and long-poll form the sync protocol; the metadata DB maps files to immutable content-addressed blocks. Magic Pocket is the erasure-coded object store that replaced S3 at exabyte scale.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why uploading whole files is the wrong shape`,body:[`Let's start with the obvious wrong answer: on every save, upload the whole file. A 1 GB design file with one byte changed means 1 GB up the wire — for every device, every time. Multiply that by millions of users and you're paying for the same bytes over and over. So the first real design move is to stop thinking in files and start thinking in blocks.`,`Here's the handshake that makes Dropbox feel instant. The client splits the file into 4 MB blocks, hashes each one with SHA-256, and sends just the hash list to the server. The server replies: 'I've got blocks a and b, I'm missing d.' Only d goes up the wire. The metadata DB then records the file as an ordered list of blocks — file at path X equals blocks [a, b, d]. The file is now a pointer, not a payload.`,`And here's the part I love: the first 100,000 users ran on one Python script and rsync. The block protocol came later. You don't need the final architecture to find out whether people want a folder that syncs — you need it the moment bandwidth becomes your biggest line item.`],quote:`The first 100k users were powered by one Python script and rsync.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Full-file upload`,`Trivially simple, no server state`,`Re-sends every byte on every edit`,`Prototype only`],[`rsync-style delta`,`Diffs against one known copy`,`Needs both versions; weak for multi-device`,`Early stage`],[`4 MB blocks + hash handshake`,`Dedup across users and devices; tiny uploads`,`Requires metadata DB and block store`,`Dropbox's answer`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Dropbox — File sync`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-dropbox.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what one inserted byte costs you`,body:[`Now walk the boundary — literally, the chunk boundaries. Fixed 4 MB chunking works beautifully until someone inserts a paragraph at the top of a document. Every byte after the insertion point shifts, so every subsequent 4 MB block hashes differently. A one-line edit just invalidated roughly 250 blocks of a 1 GB file. Your delta sync quietly became a full re-upload.`,`The fix is content-defined chunking: run a rolling hash — a Rabin fingerprint — over the byte stream, and declare a chunk boundary wherever the hash hits a magic value. Boundaries now depend on content, not offsets. Insert bytes in the middle and only the chunk containing the insertion re-hashes; everything downstream still lands on the same boundaries. The insert costs you one chunk, not the rest of the file.`,`The trade-off? Variable chunk sizes and real CPU burned on the client computing rolling hashes, plus you have to re-chunk old files to benefit. But for the workloads Dropbox cares about — documents that grow by insertion — it's not close.`],quote:`Chunk boundaries should live in the content, not in the offsets.`,table:{headers:[`Chunking`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Fixed 4 MB offsets`,`Simple, predictable sizes`,`An insert shifts every later block`,`Append-only files`],[`Content-defined (Rabin)`,`Insert invalidates only one chunk`,`Variable sizes, client CPU cost`,`Documents, real-world edits`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Dropbox — File sync`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-dropbox.html`}},{heading:`03 · The other half: how does your laptop even know?`,body:[`Uploading is half the protocol. The other half: how does your laptop in Berlin find out that your phone in Tokyo just changed the file? Polling on an interval means latency equals your poll interval, and you burn requests all day. Dropbox's answer is the long-poll: the client holds a connection open, and the server answers the moment a change lands. The control channel stays tiny; data moves by pull.`,`When the notification arrives, it's just metadata: 'file X's block list is now [a, b, d].' The client downloads only the blocks it doesn't have locally and reassembles the file. And when two devices edit offline and both sync? Dropbox refuses to pick a winner — it keeps both, renaming one a 'conflicted copy.' For files, last-write-wins is data loss wearing a tuxedo.`],quote:`Never destroy a user's work to win a merge.`,table:{headers:[`Change detection`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Interval polling`,`Dead simple`,`Latency = interval; wasted requests`,`Background jobs`],[`Long-poll`,`Near-instant; one open connection`,`Server holds connections`,`Sync clients`],[`WebSockets`,`Bidirectional, lowest latency`,`Reconnect and infra complexity`,`Chat, live collaboration`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Dropbox — File sync`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-dropbox.html`}},{heading:`04 · Magic Pocket: when 'build our own storage' stops being crazy`,body:[`Last thought, the big one. By 2015 Dropbox was storing its blocks on S3 and staring at roughly a $1B annual AWS bill. Magic Pocket was the answer: their own object storage on commodity hardware in leased datacenters. The migration took 2.5 years. Today roughly 90% of stored bytes live there, saving about $75M a year.`,`The architecture is textbook exabyte engineering: SMR (shingled magnetic recording) disks for cold storage, Reed-Solomon erasure coding at 10+4 — survive four simultaneous disk failures — and cross-region replication. Custom everything: storage software, hardware ordering, the lot. And here's why the migration didn't hurt: blocks are immutable and content-addressed, so you can copy the universe in the background and flip lookups only when a block exists in both places.`,`So when should you build your own storage? Dropbox had an exabyte, predictable access patterns, and a billion-dollar bill — the math worked. For everyone else: don't. The fixed cost is on the order of $1B in engineering and facilities; the per-GB savings only matter at exabyte scale. Magic Pocket is a case study in what's possible, not a recommendation.`],quote:`Magic Pocket is a case study in what's possible, not a recommendation.`,table:{headers:[`Option`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`AWS S3`,`Zero ops, instant scale`,`~$1B/yr at Dropbox's scale`,`Nearly everyone`],[`Magic Pocket`,`~$75M/yr savings, full control`,`2.5-yr migration, ~$1B fixed cost`,`Exabyte scale only`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Dropbox — File sync`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-dropbox.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`sync-protocol`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The Dropbox sync handshake`,caption:`One edited file becomes a hash list, a dedup check, and a handful of missing blocks — not a re-upload.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["User edits file"] --> B["Client splits into 4 MB blocks"]
    B --> C["SHA-256 hash each block"]
    C --> D["Send block hash list to server"]
    D --> E{"Server: which blocks exist?"}
    E -->|"dedup hit"| F["Skip: reference existing block"]
    E -->|"missing"| G["Upload only missing blocks"]
    F --> H["Metadata DB: file = block list a,b,d"]
    G --> H
    H --> I["Long-poll notify other devices"]
    I --> J["Peer downloads blocks it lacks"]
    J --> K["Assemble file locally"]`},{id:`magic-pocket-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Block storage topology, pre- and post-Magic Pocket`,caption:`Clients talk to API servers; metadata maps files to blocks; the block service reads and writes Magic Pocket — with S3 as the legacy path it replaced.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph Devices
        C1["Desktop client"]
        C2["Mobile client"]
    end
    C1 --> API["Dropbox API servers"]
    C2 --> API
    API --> META["Metadata DB: file to block list"]
    API --> BS["Block service"]
    BS --> MP["Magic Pocket: erasure coded 10+4"]
    MP --> SMR["SMR disks: cold storage"]
    MP --> XR["Cross-region replica"]
    BS -. legacy path .-> S3["AWS S3: pre-2016"]`}],takeaways:[`Chunk files into 4 MB blocks and SHA-256 each one: a 1-byte edit to a 1 GB file re-uploads a single block — 0.4% of the file, not 100%.`,`Switch to content-defined chunking (Rabin rolling hash) when users insert mid-file: an insert invalidates only the chunk it lands in, not the ~250 blocks after it.`,`Run the hash handshake before uploading — ask the server which blocks it already has and send only the missing ones; cross-user dedup makes shared files nearly free to store.`,`Keep the control channel tiny: long-poll for change notification (near-zero latency, no polling storm), then pull exactly the blocks you lack.`,`Build your own object storage only at exabyte scale with predictable access: Dropbox's ~$1B/yr AWS bill justified a 2.5-year migration and ~$75M/yr savings — below that, the fixed cost never pays back.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Dropbox — File sync`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-dropbox.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`},{kind:`youtube`,label:`Hello Interview — Design Dropbox or Google Drive w/ a Ex-Meta Staff Engineer System Design Interview`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_UZ1ngy-kOI`,author:`Hello Interview`,note:`Designing Dropbox or Google Drive, live.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`case-figma-postgres`,pathId:`case-studies`,order:5,title:`Figma — Scaling Postgres to 4M users`,subtitle:`Figma's database was one Postgres instance. It scaled vertically — bigger machine — for years, doubling capacity faster than they consumed it. When vertical scaling stopped paying,`,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`application sharding`,`pgbouncer`],story:{headline:`The biggest box in the catalog wasn't big enough.`,paragraphs:[`3 AM. The pager fires: primary Postgres at 95% CPU. Autovacuum on the biggest table is on day three, its IOPS fighting live user traffic for every disk cycle. Replica lag, normally ~50ms, is now measured in seconds. You open the runbook and the first line says 'upgrade the instance size' — and you realize, with a cold feeling, that you're already on the largest RDS instance AWS sells. There is no next line.`,`This was Figma in 2022. Four million users on a real-time multiplayer design tool, where every keystroke, cursor move, and shape edit is a row written to one Postgres primary. CPU had been doubling every six months — 10× a year — and vertical scaling had always outrun it: bigger machine, another year of headroom. But the math finally broke. 128 vCPUs, 1 TB of RAM, roughly $25k a month, 65% steady-state CPU with 95% spikes, and the runway measured in months.`,`The team didn't reach for NoSQL, Aurora, or Spanner. They did something more boring and more brave: Postgres → Postgres, plus a routing layer. Two rungs on a ladder — split the big tables out vertically first, then hash-shard the largest table horizontally. This is the story of what they did between those two states.`],problem:`How do you scale a single-primary Postgres growing 10× per year past the largest instance AWS sells — without giving up transactions, foreign keys, or rewriting the application?`},tech:{stack:[`Postgres`,`AWS RDS`,`pgbouncer`,`Hash Sharding`],note:`One Postgres primary behind pgbouncer carried Figma for years; the escape hatch was staying on Postgres while adding vertical partitioning, then hash-based sharding behind an application routing layer.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why not just buy a bigger box?`,body:[`Let's start with the play that had worked every single time before: buy a bigger machine. And honestly, it was a great play for years — Figma doubled capacity faster than they consumed it, and each RDS upgrade bought roughly another year of runway. If your instinct is 'vertical scaling first, rearchitect later', you're in good company. That instinct was correct right up until it wasn't.`,`Here's the catch: load was growing 10× per year — CPU doubling every six months. By 2022 they were on the largest RDS instance available: 128 vCPUs, 1 TB of RAM, about $25k a month, running 65% CPU steady-state and peaking at 95%. There is no next SKU. The lever that always worked was gone, and that's the exact moment you stop shopping and start designing.`],quote:`Vertical scaling is a subscription, not a strategy — the catalog of bigger boxes always ends.`,table:{headers:[`Option`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Bigger RDS box`,`Zero code change`,`No SKU left; ~1 yr runway at best`,`Dead end by 2022`],[`Aurora`,`Better IO, managed ops`,`Single-writer CPU ceiling remains`,`Partial relief only`],[`NoSQL rewrite`,`Elastic write scaling`,`Multi-year rewrite; loses relational model`,`Too costly`],[`Postgres + sharding`,`Keeps transactions & FKs`,`Must build a routing layer`,`Chosen path`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Figma — Scaling Postgres to 4M users`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-figma-postgres.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what is this data actually doing?`,body:[`Before reaching for any migration, ask the unglamorous question: what does the data actually want to be? Figma's world is deeply relational — files contain nodes, nodes have properties and history, teams own files. The application code assumes transactions and foreign keys everywhere. That's not an accident; that's the product.`,`So the slide-deck answers — 'go NoSQL', 'event-source everything', 'move to Spanner' — all looked correct on the slide and wrong in the implementation. Each one is a multi-year rewrite of correctness, not a weekend migration. Aurora helps with the IO bottleneck but leaves the single-writer CPU ceiling untouched. The cheapest correct move was to keep the relational contract and change the deployment topology instead. Postgres → Postgres means every guarantee you rely on survives the trip.`],quote:`You don't replace Postgres at scale — you teach the application to live with fewer assumptions about it.`,source:{label:`sharpoverride — Figma — Scaling Postgres to 4M users`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-figma-postgres.html`}},{heading:`03 · The three walls: vacuum, lag, and the read-split lie`,body:[`Three walls, in the order they hurt. Wall one: vacuum. Postgres reclaims space from dead tuples per table, and on multi-terabyte tables those vacuums took days — their IOPS competing with live traffic the whole time. A table that takes three days to vacuum cannot be the foundation of a real-time product. Splitting tables splits the vacuum cost too.`,`Wall two: replica lag — about 50ms in steady state, several seconds during write bursts. Which sets up wall three, the lie your dashboard tells you: yes, replicas absorbed roughly 70% of read load, but the ~30% of reads that need fresh data — 'show me my latest edits' — route straight back to the primary. Your replica fleet only ever offloads the stale-tolerant fraction. The primary never gets the relief the capacity graph promises.`],quote:`A replica that can't serve your freshest reads is a mirror, not capacity.`,table:{headers:[`Load class`,`Share`,`Tolerates lag?`,`Lands on`],rows:[[`Presence, history, analytics`,`~70%`,`Yes (~50ms+)`,`Read replicas`],[`Latest edits after a write`,`~30%`,`No`,`Primary`],[`Write bursts`,`—`,`n/a`,`Primary (lag spikes to seconds)`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Figma — Scaling Postgres to 4M users`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-figma-postgres.html`}},{heading:`04 · The ladder: partition vertically, then shard horizontally`,body:[`The escape had two rungs, and the order matters. Rung one: vertical partitioning — pull the largest tables out of the main database into their own per-table databases. Each one gets its own machine and its own vacuum budget, and the application learns to talk to more than one database. Here's why I love this step: no rows move. It's a full rehearsal for your routing layer with zero data-distribution risk.`,`Rung two: horizontal sharding of the largest table, split by hash of a logical 'shard ID', with a routing layer mapping shard ID → database. Because it's Postgres → Postgres, transactions and foreign keys survive inside each shard — you co-locate a file's rows on one shard so local operations stay local. No NoSQL, no Aurora, no Spanner. Just Postgres, more times, behind a smarter front door.`],quote:`Shard by the key your transactions live on — the routing layer is only as smart as the boundary you draw.`,table:{headers:[`Move`,`What it splits`,`Main risk`,`Payoff`],rows:[[`Vertical partitioning`,`Tables → own databases`,`Cross-DB joins and FKs`,`Immediate CPU + vacuum relief`],[`Horizontal sharding`,`Rows → shards by hash of shard ID`,`Routing layer, future resharding`,`Write headroom beyond any box`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Figma — Scaling Postgres to 4M users`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-figma-postgres.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`before-architecture`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Before: one primary, replicas, and the 30% that comes home`,caption:`2020–2022 topology: a single RDS primary behind pgbouncer, replicas serving stale-tolerant reads — while the ~30% of fresh reads always funnel back to the strained primary.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  C["Design clients"] --> PB["pgbouncer"]
  PB --> P[("Postgres primary<br/>128 vCPU · 1 TB RAM<br/>~$25k/mo")]
  P --> R1["Replica 1"]
  P --> R2["Replica 2"]
  P --> R3["Replica 3"]
  R1 --> LB["Replica load balancer"]
  R2 --> LB
  R3 --> LB
  C -.->|"~70% stale-tolerant reads<br/>lag ~50ms"| LB
  C -.->|"~30% fresh reads<br/>must hit primary"| P
  P -.->|"autovacuum: days on multi-TB tables<br/>IOPS vs live traffic"| V["Vacuum pressure"]`},{id:`after-sharding`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`After: routing layer + hash shards, still Postgres`,caption:`The destination state: an application routing layer hashes a logical shard ID; each shard is a small Postgres with its own replicas and vacuum budget, and a file's rows stay co-located for local transactions.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
  APP["App servers"] --> RT["Routing layer<br/>hash of logical shard ID"]
  RT -->|"shard 0"| S0[("Postgres shard 0<br/>primary + replicas")]
  RT -->|"shard 1"| S1[("Postgres shard 1<br/>primary + replicas")]
  RT -->|"shard N"| SN[("Postgres shard N<br/>primary + replicas")]
  RT --> META[("Metadata DB<br/>shard map")]
  S0 -.->|"file rows co-located:<br/>transactions + FKs intact"| L["Local guarantees"]
  S1 -.->|"small tables:<br/>vacuum in hours"| V["Vacuum budget per shard"]`}],takeaways:[`CPU doubling every 6 months means each instance upgrade buys ~12 months — start the sharding project the same day you order the bigger box.`,`When a vacuum takes 3 days on a multi-TB table, split the table; vacuum time must fit inside your maintenance window, not your SLO.`,`Budget replicas for the ~70% of reads that tolerate ~50ms staleness — the ~30% that need fresh data will always land on the primary.`,`Partition vertically before sharding horizontally: per-table databases are a zero-data-movement rehearsal for your routing layer.`,`Hash-shard by a logical shard ID that matches your transaction boundary (the file), so transactions and foreign keys survive inside each shard.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Figma — Scaling Postgres to 4M users`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-figma-postgres.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`case-google-docs`,pathId:`case-studies`,order:6,title:`Google Docs — Real-time collaboration`,subtitle:`Google Docs' real-time multiplayer document editing was a 2007 product miracle. The underlying technique — Operational Transformation — predated Docs and is still the production an`,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[],story:{headline:`Two cursors, one position, three different documents.`,paragraphs:[`Picture a sprint-planning doc at 11 PM, the night before a launch. Alice puts her cursor at position 5 of the roadmap paragraph and types 'X'. Bob, watching the same doc, puts his cursor at the exact same position and types 'Y'. Both keystrokes leave the browser within 40 milliseconds of each other. The naive backend — broadcast every op to every client, apply on arrival — happily does exactly that.`,`Alice's screen ends with '...XY...'. Bob's ends with '...YX...'. Neither notices for ten minutes. Then Carol pastes in a 'fix' based on what she sees — now a third variant — and every subsequent insert lands at an offset computed against a document that no longer exists on anyone else's screen. The doc eats itself, character by character, and the revision history becomes a crime scene.`,`The 3 AM postmortem line that stings: the system never crashed, never dropped a message, never lost an op. It delivered everything perfectly — in the wrong order. Real-time collaboration isn't a transport problem. It's a convergence problem, and it has exactly two known solutions: Operational Transformation and CRDTs.`],problem:`Two editors type at the same position simultaneously. Whose change wins? Without coordination — both. Convergence is the goal.`},tech:{stack:[`Operational Transformation`,`CRDTs`,`Central Op Server`,`Sequence Numbers`,`WebSocket Fanout`,`Yjs / Automerge`],note:`The op model (OT or CRDT) defines what an edit even is; the convergence point — a central transformer with sequence numbers, or commutative ops by construction — guarantees every replica lands on the same string; WebSockets just move the bytes.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why 'just broadcast the ops' diverges`,body:[`So let's start where everyone starts: 'I'll just broadcast the ops.' Alice types X at position 5, Bob types Y at position 5, both ops go to everyone, everyone applies them. Done, right? No. Alice applies her own X first, then Bob's Y — she sees XY. Bob applies his own Y first, then Alice's X — he sees YX. Same ops, same starting document, different endings. That's divergence, and it's fatal.`,`And here's the nasty part — it compounds. Carol's next edit is positioned against whatever she sees, which is now a third variant. Every subsequent op lands at an offset computed against a document that doesn't exist on the other screens. Cursors drift, characters migrate, and ten minutes later the paragraph is word salad. Nothing crashed, nothing was lost — the order was just wrong.`,`So the actual requirement isn't 'fast'. It's: after every op has been delivered to every replica, in any order, all replicas show the same bytes. State it that way and the design space collapses to two moves — either a coordinator rewrites ops to fit a canonical order (that's OT), or the ops are built so order never mattered (that's CRDT). Everything else is plumbing.`],quote:`Real-time collab isn't about speed. It's about converging on the same answer.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`What actually happens`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`Naive op broadcast`,`Each client applies ops in arrival order`,`Diverges on any concurrent edit`],[`Last-write-wins`,`One person's keystrokes silently vanish`,`Data loss — trust killer`],[`Lock the paragraph`,`Second typist waits or bounces`,`Not real-time anymore`],[`OT with central server`,`Server rebases ops into one canonical order`,`Converges; very hard to build`],[`CRDT`,`Ops commutative by construction`,`Converges; metadata cost`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Google Docs — Real-time collaboration`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-google-docs.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the server is the referee`,body:[`Google's answer in 2007: put a referee in the middle. Clients never talk to each other — they send ops (insert 'X' at pos 5, delete at pos 7, length 1) to a central server. The server linearizes: every op gets a sequence number, one canonical order, zero ambiguity about who went first.`,`Now the magic. When Bob's op arrives, the server checks what Bob last saw. If Alice's X-at-5 landed after Bob's last known sequence, Bob's op is stale — so the server transforms it. Y-at-5 becomes Y-at-6, because Alice's X shifted everything right by one. Transform, broadcast, and every client converges. The server isn't a database here; it's a referee rewriting every play to fit the game so far.`,`Why doesn't everyone do this? The transform functions for text are brutal. Insert and delete interact in nine different ways depending on relative positions and lengths — get one case wrong and you get silent character-eating bugs that only appear under concurrency. Google has poured years of engineering into Docs' OT engine. This is not a weekend library.`],quote:`In OT, the server isn't a database — it's a referee that rewrites every play to fit the game so far.`,table:{headers:[`Concurrent ops`,`Naive result`,`After server transform`,`Both screens show`],rows:[[`insert X@5 · insert Y@5`,`XY vs YX — divergence`,`Bob's op rebased to insert Y@6`,`One canonical order, both sides`],[`insert X@5 · delete @2`,`Edits computed against different docs`,`Insert rebased to @4 once delete lands first`,`Identical strings`],[`delete @2 · delete @2`,`Second delete hits the wrong char`,`Second delete collapses to a no-op`,`One char removed`],[`insert @5 · insert @7`,`Later positions drift by one`,`Second insert rebased to @8`,`Both chars, correct spots`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Google Docs — Real-time collaboration`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-google-docs.html`}},{heading:`03 · CRDTs: make order not matter`,body:[`CRDTs attack the problem from the other end. Instead of transforming ops to fit an order, you design ops that are commutative by construction — apply them in any order, get the same result. Convergence stops being an algorithm you run and becomes a property the data type guarantees. Mathematically. No referee required.`,`For text, that means tree-based structures — RGA, Yjs's Y-CRDT, Automerge. Every character gets a stable identifier living in a partial order; ops insert and delete by identifier, never by integer position. The IDs themselves encode the ordering, so there's nothing to transform. Alice's X and Bob's Y are just two siblings in a tree; the tie-break is baked into the data.`,`The payoff is huge: edits apply locally at zero network latency, offline editing is native, and peers can sync without any central server. The cost is metadata — every character carries an ID, every delete leaves a tombstone, so memory grows with edit history. Compression helps, but it's a real bill at scale.`],quote:`Don't transform the ops — design the data so order never mattered.`,table:{headers:[`Aspect`,`OT`,`CRDT`],rows:[[`Server required`,`Yes (central transformer)`,`No (peer-to-peer works)`],[`Complexity to implement`,`Very high`,`Moderate`],[`Memory per doc`,`Bounded`,`Grows with edits (compression mitigates)`],[`Offline edits`,`Hard`,`Native`],[`Latency per edit`,`Server round-trip`,`Local (peer sync optional)`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Google Docs — Real-time collaboration`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-google-docs.html`}},{heading:`04 · Why Google Docs never rewrote it`,body:[`Here's the part that surprises people: Figma, Linear, Tldraw, Notion's newer surfaces — they're all CRDTs now. Google Docs is still OT. And that's not fossilization; it's arithmetic.`,`Docs' OT engine has been load-bearing for nearly two decades. A rewrite touches every document, every client, every integration — and re-opens all nine of those edge cases under a brand-new model. Meanwhile, the CRDT wins (offline-first, peer-to-peer, local latency) matter least to a product whose clients are always-online browsers backed by Google-grade infrastructure. Memory growth is a genuine cost at Docs' scale.`,`The general lesson: a convergence algorithm is infrastructure, not a feature. You pick one, you live inside its constraints, and you replace it only when the product's shape changes — not when a nicer paper shows up on arXiv.`],quote:`A working OT engine is a moat; a rewrite is a bet.`,table:{headers:[`Factor`,`Stay on OT (Docs' call)`,`Rewrite to CRDT`],rows:[[`Migration risk`,`Zero — engine proven for ~20 yrs`,`Every doc, client, integration re-tested`],[`Offline editing`,`Hard to retrofit`,`Native`],[`Memory at scale`,`Bounded per doc`,`Grows with history; needs GC`],[`Edge-case risk`,`Nine known cases, all handled`,`Nine cases re-learned the hard way`],[`Net at Docs' scale`,`Keep shipping features`,`Cost outweighs benefit`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Google Docs — Real-time collaboration`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-google-docs.html`}},{heading:`05 · What I'd ship today`,body:[`So what would I actually build in this decade? Not hand-rolled OT — life's too short and those nine cases are unforgiving. Take Yjs or Automerge, or a managed collaborative-editing service. The convergence algorithm is a commodity now; your product is not.`,`The architecture I'd draw: a CRDT document living client-side, and a server that's deliberately dumb — it relays updates, persists snapshots, and runs a presence channel for cursors. The server never has to understand the ops, which shrinks the blast radius enormously compared to a transformer. If the relay hiccups, clients keep editing and sync later.`,`And test it the way you test idempotency: property-based tests that shuffle op delivery order across N replicas and assert every replica ends byte-identical. If your test suite can't express that, you don't have real-time collaboration — you have real-time divergence with good marketing.`],quote:`Buy the convergence algorithm. Build the product. Test the shuffling.`,table:{headers:[`Layer`,`Build it?`,`Adopt instead`],rows:[[`Convergence engine`,`Only if you're Google`,`Yjs, Automerge, ShareDB`],[`Transport + relay`,`Thin WebSocket fanout`,`y-websocket, PartyKit`],[`Presence / cursors`,`Small awareness channel`,`Built into Yjs awareness`],[`Persistence`,`Snapshots + incremental updates`,`y-indexeddb, LevelDB adapters`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Google Docs — Real-time collaboration`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-google-docs.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`ot-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`OT hub-and-spoke: the server is the convergence point`,caption:`Clients never talk to each other. The server linearizes, transforms each op against what that client last saw, and fans out — one canonical order, zero ambiguity.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
  subgraph Clients["Browser clients"]
    A["Alice editor"]
    B["Bob editor"]
    C["Carol editor"]
  end
  A -- "op: insert X at 5" --> S["Central OT server"]
  B -- "op: insert Y at 5" --> S
  C -- "op: delete at 2, len 1" --> S
  S --> Q["1. Linearize - assign sequence numbers"]
  Q --> T["2. Transform vs ops since client's last seq"]
  T --> L[("Op log / persistence")]
  T --> F["3. Broadcast transformed ops"]
  F --> A
  F --> B
  F --> C`},{id:`ot-transform-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The diamond: two inserts at position 5`,caption:`Alice and Bob type at the same position. The server rebases Bob's stale op, and both screens land on the same string — HelloXY — without losing a single keystroke.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant A as Alice
  participant S as OT server
  participant B as Bob
  Note over A,B: Both see doc: Hello
  A->>S: insert X at pos 5
  Note over S: linearize - assign server seq 42
  S->>B: broadcast insert X at 5
  B->>S: insert Y at pos 5 (last seen seq 41)
  Note over S: Bob is 1 op stale - transform Y at 5 against X at 5 gives Y at 6
  S->>A: broadcast insert Y at 6
  S->>B: insert X at 5 (transformed vs Bob's pending Y)
  Note over A: Hello becomes HelloX then HelloXY
  Note over B: Hello becomes HelloY then HelloXY
  Note over A,B: Same string on both screens - convergence`}],takeaways:[`Pick ONE canonical order before you ship: server sequence numbers (OT) or commutative op IDs (CRDT). Two clients applying the same ops in different orders is divergence, not collaboration.`,`Text OT has ~9 distinct insert/delete interaction cases — hand-rolling it is a multi-year project. Adopt a proven engine (ShareDB, Yjs) unless you are Google.`,`CRDT memory grows with edit history — every character carries an ID and every delete a tombstone; plan compression/GC before a hot doc crosses ~100k ops.`,`Latency math: OT pays ~1 server round-trip per op; CRDTs apply locally at ~0 ms and sync async — your offline requirement picks the algorithm for you.`,`Google Docs kept OT load-bearing for ~2 decades because rewrite cost > benefit — treat a working convergence engine as infrastructure, not a refactor target.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Google Docs — Real-time collaboration`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-google-docs.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`},{kind:`youtube`,label:`Hello Interview — Design Dropbox or Google Drive w/ a Ex-Meta Staff Engineer System Design Interview`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_UZ1ngy-kOI`,author:`Hello Interview`,note:`Designing Dropbox or Google Drive, live.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`case-hotstar-concurrent`,pathId:`case-studies`,order:7,title:`Hotstar — 25M concurrent`,subtitle:`Disney+ Hotstar holds the world record for concurrent streaming viewers — 59 million during the 2023 ICC World Cup. The previous record (its own, 2019) was 25 million. The architec`,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[`CDN sharding`,`graceful degradation`,`pre-warmed capacity`],story:{headline:`We knew the exact minute. That was the terrifying part.`,paragraphs:[`Picture the war room an hour before the 2023 World Cup final. Not a firefighting scene — a launch countdown. Every server that will serve this match is already running, already paid for, already warm. The dashboards are green and boring, and that's the point: by the time things get exciting, it's too late to do anything about it.`,`T-0. The toss happens, and the graph goes vertical: zero to 25 million concurrent viewers in under five minutes. Pods take ~30 seconds to cold-start, VMs ~3 minutes, a new region ~30 — the spike finishes before any of them would have finished booting. The only reason the video doesn't stutter is that everything the first 25 million viewers will touch was pre-cached, pre-minted, and pre-reserved an hour earlier.`,`By full time the counter reads 59 million concurrent — a world record, roughly 236 Tbps of egress from a single event, in a country where three ISPs carry most of the audience. The lesson isn't elasticity. For a spike with a known minute, you don't scale up. You show up already scaled.`],problem:`How do you absorb a 0→25M-concurrent ramp in under five minutes when auto-scaling needs minutes just to boot — and the spike's start time is printed on the calendar months in advance?`},tech:{stack:[`CDN Sharding`,`Pre-warmed Capacity`,`HLS Edge Cache`,`Graceful Degradation`],note:`Pre-warmed compute and edge-cached HLS chunks carry minute zero; ISP-embedded CDN shards keep bytes off the public internet; a pre-agreed degradation matrix protects video as the only P0.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why auto-scaling loses the clock`,body:[`Let's start with the mental model we have to break. Most of us learned scaling as a reactive loop: load rises, metrics fire, platform adds capacity. That loop has a clock, and the clock is slower than a cricket match. A Kubernetes pod cold-starts in about 30 seconds. A new VM, three minutes. A whole new region, thirty if everything goes right. Now look at the workload: zero to 25 million concurrent viewers in under five minutes, at a minute the entire country knows in advance.`,`So when someone asks 'why didn't they just auto-scale?' — the honest answer is that auto-scaling was still booting when the match started. The Hotstar pattern inverts it: capacity is reserved and running before kick-off. Auto-scaling isn't deleted, it's demoted — it exists only to handle the upside surprise, the surge beyond the forecast. The forecast itself is met by capacity you pre-positioned.`],quote:`Elastic scale is a marketing term. Live sports is the workload that proves it false.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Auto-scaling`,`Cheap at idle, no waste`,`Minutes of lag, cold-start storms`,`Upside surprise only`],[`Pre-warmed reserve`,`Capacity at minute zero`,`Pays for idle hours`,`Known-time mega events`],[`Hybrid reserve + elastic`,`Covers forecast and surprise`,`Complex runbooks`,`Hotstar's actual model`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Hotstar — 25M concurrent`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-hotstar-concurrent.html`}},{heading:`02 · Second thought: pre-positioning the first minute`,body:[`Here's the move that saves your origin. An hour before the match, the first 30 seconds of the broadcast — the opening credits, the toss — are pushed into cache at every POP. So when 25 million playlist requests land in the opening minute, they all get the same cached chunks. No origin fetch, no cold-start stampede. Without this, you could reserve all the compute in the world and the origin would still melt in the first 60 seconds, because everything misses cache at once.`,`Same discipline on the auth path: tokens are short-lived and pre-minted, so the login wall doesn't become the second bottleneck right behind the video. Notice the pattern — it's not 'add more servers.' It's 'remove the work that 25 million people would otherwise all do at the same instant.' Pre-positioning is really deduplication of the first minute.`],quote:`The spike doesn't meet a system that scales up. It meets a system that was already there.`,table:{headers:[`Pre-warmed asset`,`When`,`Why`],rows:[[`First 30s segments at POPs`,`T-60 min`,`Zero origin fetch at open`],[`Short-lived auth tokens`,`T-60 min`,`Login wall doesn't melt`],[`Reserved compute pool`,`T-60 min`,`Capacity exists before demand`],[`Degradation switches armed`,`T-30 min`,`One flag flip, not a deploy`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Hotstar — 25M concurrent`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-hotstar-concurrent.html`}},{heading:`03 · Third thought: sharding the CDN by ISP`,body:[`Third thought: where do the bytes actually travel? India's audience concentrates through a handful of giant ISPs — Jio, Airtel, BSNL. Instead of letting traffic fight across the public internet, Hotstar peers directly with each ISP and serves from POPs physically inside the ISP's network. The last mile stays in the ISP's own backbone. That's cheaper transit, lower latency, and — the part people forget — fewer congested hops where 25 million flows can collide.`,`There's a resilience bonus too: sharding by ISP gives you blast-radius isolation, so a problem in one POP shard doesn't take down the whole delivery layer. And it changes the negotiation — when you're a top-three traffic partner for an ISP, capacity conversations go very differently than when you're just another origin behind transit.`],quote:`Don't fight the public internet for the last mile — move into the ISP's basement.`,table:{headers:[`Delivery path`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Public transit CDN`,`Any ISP, zero deals`,`Transit cost, jitter, congestion`,`Long-tail audiences`],[`ISP-embedded POPs`,`In-backbone bytes, cheap, low latency`,`Peering ops per ISP`,`Top 2-3 ISPs = most of India`],[`Multi-CDN + RUM steering`,`Resilience, leverage`,`Complexity, steering lag`,`Global-scale events`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Hotstar — 25M concurrent`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-hotstar-concurrent.html`}},{heading:`04 · Fourth thought: degrade on purpose, not by accident`,body:[`Fourth thought, and this one is as much product as engineering: not everything has to survive the spike. Video is the only P0. Comments, chat, emoji reactions, watch-party — all optional, all with pre-agreed kill switches. When load crosses a tier, you flip a flag and those features disappear; the video keeps playing. Bitrate downgrades come before drops, so quality degrades on a curve instead of falling off a cliff.`,`The crucial detail is when that matrix was written: weeks before match day, agreed with the product team in daylight. If you're deciding what to break at 11pm during a match, you've already lost — that's an incident, not an architecture. A degradation matrix is an artefact you ship, version, and rehearse, like a runbook.`],quote:`Graceful degradation is a product decision made in daylight, executed by a flag flip at night.`,table:{headers:[`Feature`,`Priority`,`What degrades first`],rows:[[`Video playback`,`P0 — critical`,`Bitrate downgrades before drops`],[`Login / sign-up`,`P0 — critical`,`Tokens cached aggressively`],[`Player controls`,`P1 — important`,`Seek may stall`],[`Comments / chat`,`P2 — optional`,`Disabled at overload`],[`Emoji reactions`,`P2 — optional`,`Disabled at overload`],[`Watch-party`,`P2 — optional`,`Disabled at overload`],[`Recommendations`,`P2 — optional`,`Cached static list`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Hotstar — 25M concurrent`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-hotstar-concurrent.html`}},{heading:`05 · Fifth thought: the 236 Tbps reality check`,body:[`Last thought — let's do the scary arithmetic out loud. 59 million concurrent viewers times an average 4 Mbps is roughly 236 Tbps of peak egress, from one event. For scale: Netflix's global peak sits in similar territory, and Hotstar hits it during a single cricket match. Numbers like that mean your capacity plan is, to first order, your bitrate ladder.`,`That's why degradation starts with bitrate downgrades rather than dropping viewers. Nudge the average from 4 Mbps to 2.5 and you've cut egress by nearly 40% — more headroom than any amount of emergency provisioning could buy you in five minutes. Every optional feature you disable is connections and bytes you never spend. At this scale, the levers are math, not heroics.`],quote:`At 59 million concurrent, the bitrate ladder is your capacity plan.`,table:{headers:[`Avg bitrate`,`Egress at 59M`,`Relative load`],rows:[[`4.0 Mbps`,`~236 Tbps`,`100%`],[`3.0 Mbps`,`~177 Tbps`,`75%`],[`2.5 Mbps`,`~148 Tbps`,`63%`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Hotstar — 25M concurrent`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-hotstar-concurrent.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Match-day delivery topology`,caption:`Viewers are served from POPs embedded inside their own ISP's backbone; a control plane holds the pre-agreed kill switches over every shard.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  subgraph V["Viewers 0-59M"]
    A["Jio subscribers"]
    B["Airtel subscribers"]
    C["Other ISPs"]
  end
  subgraph E["ISP-embedded CDN shards"]
    J["Jio POP shard"]
    Ai["Airtel POP shard"]
    N["Neutral POP shard"]
  end
  subgraph K["Pre-warmed core"]
    O["Video origin"]
    T["Auth token service"]
    D["Degradation control plane"]
  end
  A --> J
  B --> Ai
  C --> N
  J --> O
  Ai --> O
  N --> O
  A --> T
  B --> T
  C --> T
  D -.->|kill switches| J
  D -.-> Ai
  D -.-> N
  D -.-> T`},{id:`timeline`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`T-60 to full time`,caption:`The operational sequence: pre-warm an hour out, absorb the 0→25M ramp on cached chunks and cached tokens, then flip degradation tiers as load crosses thresholds.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant Ops as War room
  participant Edge as CDN POPs
  participant Auth as Auth service
  participant Usr as 25M viewers
  Note over Ops,Auth: T-60 min — pre-warm
  Ops->>Edge: Push first 30s segments and playlists to cache
  Ops->>Auth: Pre-mint short-lived tokens
  Note over Usr: T-0 — match starts
  Usr->>Edge: HLS playlist and segment requests
  Edge-->>Usr: Cached chunks, zero origin fetch
  Usr->>Auth: Token refresh
  Auth-->>Usr: Cached tokens
  Note over Ops: Load crosses tier-1 threshold
  Ops->>Edge: Disable chat, emoji, watch-party
  Ops->>Edge: Step ABR ladder down
  Note over Usr: Video keeps playing`}],takeaways:[`If the ramp is faster than your cold-start (0→25M in <5 min vs ~30s pods / ~3-min VMs), auto-scaling is decoration — reserve and pre-warm at T-60, and demote autoscaling to upside-surprise duty.`,`Pre-cache the first 30 seconds of the stream at every POP before the event: the opening minute must produce zero origin fetches, or your origin melts no matter how much compute you reserved.`,`Peer directly with the 2-3 ISPs that carry the bulk of your audience — bytes inside the ISP backbone beat bytes on public transit for cost, latency, and blast-radius isolation.`,`Write the degradation matrix in daylight, weeks ahead: video is the only P0, everything else gets a pre-agreed kill switch — deciding what to break mid-match means you've already lost.`,`Do the egress math before kick-off: concurrents × average bitrate (59M × 4 Mbps ≈ 236 Tbps) — and remember the ABR ladder is your biggest lever, worth ~40% egress savings before you touch a server.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Hotstar — 25M concurrent`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-hotstar-concurrent.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`case-instagram-scale`,pathId:`case-studies`,order:8,title:`Instagram — 2.5B users`,subtitle:`Instagram went from launch (2010) to a billion users on a Python monolith. Three engineers built the original. The story has become folklore — and a case study in how far "boring t`,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[],story:{headline:`The night one celebrity post turned a single photo into ten million writes`,paragraphs:[`It's 2012. Instagram is thirteen engineers serving tens of millions of users on a single Django monolith — and at 3 a.m. the pager goes off. A celebrity account with millions of followers just posted a photo. Fan-out-on-write means the system is trying to insert that one post into millions of follower feeds simultaneously. The queue backs up, workers pile up, Postgres replication lag climbs, and every feed in the app goes stale while the dashboards bleed red.`,`The war room splits. Half the internet is screaming the obvious answer: rewrite it in Java, go full NoSQL, start over. The team asks a narrower question instead: which component actually failed? Not Django — the app tier was fine. Not Postgres — the writes were fine. The fan-out strategy failed: push-to-every-follower collapses when one node in the social graph has millions of edges. The fix wasn't a rewrite; it was flipping celebrities to pull-on-read and keeping push for everyone else.`,`The same instinct carried the whole company. Instead of migrating off Postgres, they sharded it with a 64-bit ID scheme — 41 bits of timestamp, 13 bits of logical shard, 10 bits of sequence — so any row's home is computable from its key alone. The monolith lived on to a billion users. The rewrite meeting never happened.`],problem:`How do you scale a three-engineer Django monolith to a billion users without rewriting it — and what breaks first when a celebrity posts to millions of followers?`},tech:{stack:[`Django`,`gunicorn`,`Postgres`,`64-bit Shard IDs`,`Memcached`,`Redis`,`Cassandra`,`Hybrid Feed Fan-out`,`EC2`],note:`The boring stack that carried a billion users: a Django/gunicorn app tier, Postgres sharded via self-routing 64-bit IDs, Memcached as the read path, Redis and Cassandra where workloads outgrew Postgres, and a hybrid feed fan-out — all on plain EC2.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why 'rewrite it in Java' was the wrong instinct`,body:[`So the system is groaning and the room says 'rewrite it.' Let me push back on that reflex for a second. Instagram's team did the opposite: they inventoried what actually hurt. Django wasn't the bottleneck — the data access patterns were. So the moves were boring: add a cache layer, add read replicas, shard the database, add a queue. Each move bought one to two years of runway, and the next move was scheduled before the current one ran out.`,`Why not rewrite? Because a rewrite spends your best engineers re-implementing what already works, freezes feature work for months, and trades known problems for unknown ones. Optimizing the monolith is reversible — you can stop, measure, and change course. A rewrite is a one-way bet you can't take back mid-flight.`,`And notice the sequencing: Cassandra only entered the picture for inbox and feed after Django and Postgres were genuinely exhausted. New tech was the reward for outgrowing old tech, not the cure for feeling anxious about it.`],quote:`Boring tech scales further than the tech blogs suggest — most teams give up on it too early.`,table:{headers:[`Move`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Rewrite in new stack`,`Clean slate, modern runtime`,`Months of frozen features, new unknowns`,`Last resort`],[`Add cache layer`,`Cheap, reversible, huge read win`,`Invalidation complexity`,`First move`],[`Read replicas`,`Scales reads, low risk`,`Replication lag`,`Early move`],[`Shard Postgres`,`Scales writes and storage`,`Cross-shard queries get hard`,`When one box hurts`],[`Add Cassandra`,`Scales write-heavy inbox/feed`,`New ops burden, eventual consistency`,`Only after exhausting Postgres`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Instagram — 2.5B users`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-instagram-scale.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the 64-bit ID that made sharding cheap`,body:[`Here's the scariest question in sharding: which machine owns this row? Instagram's answer is my favorite trick in the whole story — don't look it up, compute it. Every primary key is 64 bits: 41 bits of millisecond timestamp since a custom epoch, 13 bits of logical shard ID, 10 bits of auto-increment sequence. Read the shard bits, route the row. No lookup table, no extra network hop, no central ID service to fall over.`,`Why not the alternatives? Plain auto-increment is per-database, so it collides the moment you split writes. UUIDs need no coordination but bloat every index at 128 bits, arrive in random order, and tell you nothing about location. The Instagram ID is compact, time-ordered — which keeps indexes and feeds happy — and self-routing.`,`The second half of the trick: 8192 logical shards but far fewer physical machines, each hosting many logical shards. Resharding becomes 'move logical shards between machines' — and not a single application ID changes. That's the difference between a migration and re-keying your entire dataset.`],quote:`Encode the routing in the key itself, and the database stops being a mystery.`,table:{headers:[`ID scheme`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Per-DB auto-increment`,`Tiny, ordered`,`Collides across shards, no routing info`,`Single node only`],[`UUID (128-bit)`,`No coordination needed`,`Index bloat, random order, no shard info`,`Distributed, location-agnostic`],[`Central ID service`,`Ordered, compact`,`Extra hop, single point of failure`,`Medium scale`],[`Instagram 64-bit`,`Self-routing, time-ordered, compact`,`Logical shard count capped at 8192`,`Sharded at scale`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Instagram — 2.5B users`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-instagram-scale.html`}},{heading:`03 · The feed: where fan-out-on-write hits the celebrity wall`,body:[`Now the home feed: show me posts from the people I follow. The naive answer is fan-out-on-write — when you post, write that post into every follower's feed inbox. For a user with 300 followers, that's 300 writes, fine. For a celebrity with millions of followers, one photo becomes a write storm. That's the 3 a.m. pager from the story, by the way — this failure mode is real, not hypothetical.`,`The opposite extreme is fan-out-on-read: compute the feed when the user opens the app. Cheap on write, brutal on read — because reads happen millions of times per second and every one of them pays the merge cost. Instagram landed on the hybrid: regular users fan out at post time, celebrities are pulled and merged at read time. One celebrity post is stored once, not ten million times.`,`In front of all of it sits Memcached — thousands of nodes, sharded by key, serving the feed. Hit rate is the engineering metric here, because a miss means expensive recomputation and a database hit. At this scale the cache isn't an optimization layered on top; it effectively is the read path.`],quote:`Push for the many, pull for the few — scale lives in the hybrid.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Fan-out on write`,`Fast reads, feed precomputed`,`Write storm for huge accounts`,`Regular users`],[`Fan-out on read`,`Cheap writes`,`Expensive, slow reads at scale`,`Tiny graphs, cold feeds`],[`Hybrid (Instagram)`,`Bounded writes and bounded reads`,`Merge logic at read time`,`Mixed follower graph`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Instagram — 2.5B users`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-instagram-scale.html`}},{heading:`04 · The discipline: runway clocks and reversible bets`,body:[`Zoom out and the pattern is almost boring in its discipline: measure what actually hurts, apply the smallest reversible fix, buy one to two years of runway, schedule the next move in advance. Nothing was adopted because a conference talk was exciting. Cassandra showed up only when inbox and feed workloads genuinely outgrew what Postgres wanted to do.`,`And the meta-lesson: thirteen engineers served tens of millions of users at acquisition on this stack. Architecture beats headcount, and restraint beats novelty. So the next time your database groans at 3 a.m., don't ask 'what should we migrate to?' Ask 'which specific access pattern broke — and what's the smallest reversible fix for it?'`],quote:`The rewrite meeting never happened — and that was the best architectural decision of the decade.`,source:{label:`sharpoverride — Instagram — 2.5B users`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-instagram-scale.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`sharding-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Sharding topology: 64-bit IDs route to logical shards`,caption:`Any row's home is computable from its key: 13 shard bits pick one of 8192 logical shards, many per physical machine — resharding moves shards, never re-keys rows.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["Django app<br/>gunicorn workers"] --> B["Generate 64-bit ID<br/>41b time / 13b shard / 10b seq"]
    B --> C["Route: shard = bits 41-53<br/>8192 logical shards"]
    C --> D["Logical-to-physical map"]
    D --> P1["Postgres cluster 1<br/>logical shards 0-2047"]
    D --> P2["Postgres cluster 2<br/>logical shards 2048-4095"]
    D --> P3["Postgres cluster 3<br/>logical shards 4096-6143"]
    D --> P4["Postgres cluster 4<br/>logical shards 6144-8191"]
    P1 --> R1["Read replicas"]
    P2 --> R2["Read replicas"]
    P3 --> R3["Read replicas"]
    P4 --> R4["Read replicas"]
    A -.-> MC["Memcached<br/>thousands of nodes, sharded by key"]
    A -.-> CA["Cassandra<br/>inbox / feed"]`},{id:`hybrid-feed`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Hybrid feed: push for regular users, pull for celebrities`,caption:`A regular post fans out to follower inboxes once; a celebrity post is stored once and merged into feeds at read time — no million-row write storm.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant Reg as Regular user
    participant Cel as Celebrity
    participant API as Django API
    participant Q as Fan-out queue
    participant MC as Memcached
    participant PG as Postgres shards
    Note over Reg: 300 followers
    Note over Cel: 10M followers
    Reg->>API: post photo
    API->>PG: insert post to own shard
    API->>Q: enqueue fan-out job
    Q->>MC: push post ID into 300 follower feeds
    Cel->>API: post photo
    API->>PG: insert post once - no fan-out
    Note over API,PG: reader opens home feed
    API->>MC: GET feed cache
    alt cache hit
        MC-->>API: precomputed feed list
    else cache miss
        API->>PG: fetch followees' recent posts
        API->>PG: pull celebrity's latest posts
        API->>MC: warm cache
    end
    API-->>Reg: merged feed`}],takeaways:[`Exhaust the boring stack before adopting anything new: cache → replicas → shard → queue bought Instagram 1–2 years of runway per move.`,`Encode the shard in the key: 41 bits time + 13 bits shard + 10 bits sequence = 8192 logical shards, and any row's home is computable with no lookup table.`,`Keep logical shards (8192) far above physical machine count, so resharding is a data move and application IDs never change.`,`Fan out on write for normal users, pull on read for celebrity accounts — one post to 10M followers must never trigger 10M writes.`,`Track cache hit rate as your primary scaling metric: at feed scale, a Memcached miss means recomputation, and misses dominate your latency budget.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Instagram — 2.5B users`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-instagram-scale.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`case-paypal-actor`,pathId:`case-studies`,order:9,title:`PayPal — 1B transactions, 8 VMs`,subtitle:`PayPal's payment-flow service handled a billion transactions a day on eight VMs. The trick wasn't bigger hardware — it was the actor model. Same hardware, different concurrency pri`,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[`Akka`,`Scala`,`actor model`],story:{headline:`Ten thousand threads, and every one of them asleep`,paragraphs:["It's the kind of graph that ruins a Friday: concurrent payments climbing toward peak traffic, and the thread count climbing faster. Someone grabs a thread dump off one of the payment-flow VMs — and there it is. Thousands of threads, all parked in the exact same frame: `socketRead0`, waiting on the fraud service, the ledger, a partner bank. The CPUs are barely doing work. They're mostly just switching between ten thousand sleeping threads.",`The math is brutal once you say it out loud. Every payment touches 5-10 downstream services, each call 10-100ms of pure I/O wait. Thread-per-request means a Java thread is held hostage the entire time — roughly 1 MB of stack each. Ten thousand concurrent payments is ten gigabytes of sleeping stacks, plus a GC that pauses longer every week under thread-churn pressure. The answer so far had been 'add VMs.' The fleet was at fifty and the graph was still going the wrong way.`,`So the team stopped asking 'how much bigger?' and started asking 'why is a thread asleep?' The answer — a concurrency model where nothing ever waits — took the same workload from fifty VMs to eight. Same hardware. Different primitive. That's what we're walking through today.`],problem:`Why did a billion transactions a day need ~50 VMs when the CPUs were mostly idle — and what does it actually take to run the same workload on 8?`},tech:{stack:[`Akka`,`Scala`,`Actor Model`,`Java / Tomcat`,`Thread-per-Request`],note:`The before-and-after concurrency machinery: a Java/Tomcat tier that parked a ~1 MB OS thread per in-flight payment, with the hottest, most I/O-bound path rewritten as Scala actors on Akka — mailboxes and a small dispatcher instead of ten thousand sleeping threads.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why can't we just buy more threads?`,body:["Let's do the math out loud, because this is where everyone gets trapped. A payment at PayPal fans out to 5-10 downstream services — fraud, ledger, risk scoring, partner banks, notifications. Each one is a synchronous HTTP call taking 10-100ms. Under thread-per-request, your Tomcat thread sits parked in `socketRead0` for essentially that entire duration. At 10k concurrent payments you've allocated 10k threads — about 1 MB of stack apiece, so 10 GB of memory for threads that are asleep. And the CPU? It's spending its life context-switching between idle threads, while GC pauses stretch under all the thread churn.",`So why not the standard fixes? A bigger thread pool just means more sleeping stacks and more switching — you've made the waiting room larger, not faster. Async I/O with futures is better: it buys you a real 2-3×. But you're still holding per-request bookkeeping, and under a 5-10× fan-out the pool is still the ceiling. PayPal needed a 10× improvement, not a 3× one. When tactical fixes top out an order of magnitude below the target, the concurrency model itself is the thing to change.`],quote:`A thread is 1 MB of stack. A million concurrent requests don't fit in any machine you can buy.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Bigger thread pool`,`Zero code change`,`More stacks, more context switching, GC pressure`,`2× at best`],[`Async futures / callbacks`,`Threads stay busier`,`Callback nesting, per-request bookkeeping remains`,`2-3×`],[`Actor model`,`Threads ≈ CPU cores, state isolated per actor`,`New mental model, verbose message types`,`10×+`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — PayPal — 1B transactions, 8 VMs`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-paypal-actor.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: messages, not method calls`,body:[`Here's the actual shift, and it's smaller than it sounds: an actor receives a message, processes it, sends messages, and returns. It never blocks. When the payment actor needs the fraud verdict, it fires off a message and immediately goes back to its mailbox to handle other payments. When the reply lands, it picks up right where it left off. Your thread pool shrinks to roughly the number of CPU cores — and every one of those threads is doing real work, never sleeping on I/O.`,`And there's a bonus nobody puts on the slide: isolated mutable state. Each actor owns its state privately; only that actor mutates it, in response to messages. No locks, no race conditions, no memory-model debates. If you know Node's event loop or Go's goroutines, this is the same family — the actor model adds the state-isolation guarantee on top. The trade is verbosity: every interaction becomes a message type with a handler, and you'll feel that in the code review.`],quote:`Blocking waits rent a whole thread per request; a mailbox shares one thread across thousands.`,table:{headers:[`Dimension`,`Thread-per-request`,`Actors`],rows:[[`Concurrency unit`,`OS thread (~1 MB stack)`,`Mailbox entry (bytes)`],[`Waiting on I/O`,`Parks the thread`,`Never blocks — actor returns to mailbox`],[`Shared state`,`Locks and memory-model care`,`Private per actor, no locks`],[`Failure handling`,`Exception in the call stack`,`Supervision and restart policies`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — PayPal — 1B transactions, 8 VMs`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-paypal-actor.html`}},{heading:`03 · The honest invoice: what the actor model costs you`,body:[`I'm not going to sell you this as free, because it isn't. Every interaction is now a message type with a handler — the code gets verbose, fast. You lose the call stack: debugging means tracing message flows through mailboxes, not stepping through frames in an IDE. And the runtime problems the thread model handled implicitly — backpressure, dead letters, what happens when a reply never comes — are now explicitly yours to design. Bounded mailboxes and supervision strategies aren't optional extras; they're the price of admission.`,`Testing gets harder too. Race conditions don't vanish — they move into message ordering, which is much sneakier than a contended lock. So the payoff has to be big enough to justify all of this. For PayPal's hot path, fifty VMs collapsing to eight was obviously big enough. For your internal admin CRUD panel, it never will be. Match the model to the pain.`],quote:`You trade a stack trace for a mailbox — make sure the mailbox is the easier thing to reason about.`,table:{headers:[`You gain`,`You pay`],rows:[[`5-6× density on I/O-bound paths`,`Verbose message types and handlers`],[`No locks, no races on actor state`,`No call stack — you trace messages instead`],[`Tiny fixed thread pool (~core count)`,`You own backpressure, dead letters, delivery semantics`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — PayPal — 1B transactions, 8 VMs`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-paypal-actor.html`}},{heading:`04 · The deployment move: rewrite the hot path, not the world`,body:[`This is the part I want you to steal. PayPal did not rewrite the platform on Akka. They identified the single highest-volume, most-I/O-bound path — payment authorisation — and rewrote just that, in Scala on Akka. Everything else stayed on Java/Tomcat. The two systems coexisted: the actor-based service absorbed the firehose, the Java tier handled everything with a normal request rate.`,`That containment is the whole trick. Actor code demands a different mental model and a different toolchain; imposing it across an entire engineering organisation would have been a multi-year project with a big-bang cutover at the end. Instead: one component, one team, one measurable win — fifty VMs down to eight — with a clean boundary you can roll back if it misbehaves. Rewrite the path that pays the bills, not the codebase that pays the salaries.`],quote:`Rewrite the path that pays the bills, not the codebase that pays the salaries.`,table:{headers:[`Hot-path criterion`,`Why it matters`],rows:[[`Highest volume`,`The density win compounds on every single request`],[`Most I/O-bound`,`Biggest pool of idle threads to reclaim`],[`Clean boundary`,`Coexists with the legacy tier, rollback is possible`],[`Single team owner`,`Contains the new mental model to one group`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — PayPal — 1B transactions, 8 VMs`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-paypal-actor.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`before-after-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Same workload, two concurrency models`,caption:`Thread-per-request parks a ~1 MB OS thread per in-flight payment across 5-10 blocking calls; the actor model runs the same load on a dispatcher sized to the CPU cores.`,mermaid:`flowchart TB
  subgraph BEFORE["Before — thread-per-request, ~50 VMs"]
    direction TB
    R1["10k concurrent payments"] --> P1["Tomcat pool: 10k threads × 1MB stack"]
    P1 -->|"blocking 10-100ms"| F1["Fraud"]
    P1 -->|"blocking"| L1["Ledger"]
    P1 -->|"blocking"| B1["Banks / risk / notify"]
  end
  subgraph AFTER["After — Akka actors, 8 VMs"]
    direction TB
    R2["10k concurrent payments"] --> D2["Dispatcher: ~core-count threads"]
    D2 --> M2["Payment actor + mailbox"]
    M2 -->|"async msg"| F2["Fraud"]
    M2 -->|"async msg"| L2["Ledger"]
    M2 -->|"async msg"| B2["Banks / risk / notify"]
    F2 -->|"reply"| M2
    L2 -->|"reply"| M2
    B2 -->|"reply"| M2
  end
  BEFORE -.->|"same workload, 6× fewer VMs"| AFTER`},{id:`actor-payment-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One payment through the actor flow`,caption:`The payment actor fires all downstream checks as messages and returns to its mailbox immediately — the dispatcher thread is never held hostage while fraud, ledger, and the bank think.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant GW as Gateway
  participant PA as Payment Actor
  participant FR as Fraud
  participant LG as Ledger
  participant BK as Partner Bank
  GW->>PA: Authorize payment
  Note over PA: actor mutates private state,<br/>no locks held
  PA->>FR: risk check msg
  PA->>LG: reserve funds msg
  PA->>BK: auth request msg
  Note over PA: actor returns to mailbox —<br/>thread freed for other actors
  FR-->>PA: verdict
  LG-->>PA: reserved
  BK-->>PA: auth result
  PA-->>GW: authorization complete`}],takeaways:[`A thread costs ~1 MB of stack: 10k concurrent requests means ~10 GB of sleeping stacks before any real work happens. Budget concurrency in megabytes, not requests.`,`If a thread dump shows most threads parked in socket reads, they're idle 90%+ of their life — 5-10 downstream calls × 10-100 ms each adds up fast. That's an architecture problem; more VMs just relocate it.`,`Tactical fixes (bigger pools, async futures) buy 2-3×. When the roadmap needs 10×, change the concurrency model — PayPal's actor rewrite took the same workload from ~50 VMs to 8, a 5-6× density gain.`,`Actors replace locks with mailboxes: per-actor private state means zero lock contention, but every interaction becomes a message type plus a handler — budget for the verbosity before you commit.`,`Rewrite the hot path, not the platform: PayPal converted only payment authorisation to Akka/Scala and left Java/Tomcat for everything else. Contain the new mental model to the component that pays for it.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — PayPal — 1B transactions, 8 VMs`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-paypal-actor.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`case-shopify-flash`,pathId:`case-studies`,order:10,title:`Shopify — Flash sales`,subtitle:`Shopify's Black Friday peak was 32 million requests per minute in 2022. The architecture serving it is a Ruby on Rails modular monolith, deployed in shards Shopify calls "pods", or`,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[`Cell architecture`,`Kafka`,`Pods`,`Rails monolith`],story:{headline:`Midnight on Black Friday: 533,000 requests per second, and the pager stays quiet.`,paragraphs:[`Black Friday, 12:01 AM Eastern. Checkout traffic is climbing toward 32 million requests a minute — roughly 533,000 requests per second, more than $1M of GMV clearing in a single minute, and on the order of 30 TB of data a minute flowing through the monolith. On the capacity wall, nobody is staring at one global number. They're staring at hundreds of pod graphs, because that's where the truth lives.`,`Minutes in, one merchant's product drop catches fire on social media and their store's traffic explodes. Pod 42's CPU bends upward. Every other pod's graph doesn't move a pixel. The per-store rate limiter clamps the spike, the webhook and email queues swell and start draining, and load-shedding tiers quietly drop recommendations and swap live inventory previews for stale cache. Checkout stays green. The spike burns out inside the cell it was born in — most of the platform never learns it happened.`,`Now the part that should break your brain: the thing absorbing all of this is a Ruby on Rails modular monolith — the same codebase in every pod, each pod with its own MySQL primary, its own Redis, its own background workers. No microservices rewrite. No database migration. No heroic re-architecture before the biggest shopping weekend of the year. The bet that paid off wasn't 'distribute everything.' It was 'isolate the tenant, keep the hot path local, and make moving merchants between pods a boring, automated Tuesday task.'`],problem:`Any of hundreds of thousands of stores can go viral at any minute — how do you let one tenant spike without its traffic ever touching another tenant's compute?`},tech:{stack:[`Rails monolith`,`Cell architecture`,`Pods`,`Request router`,`Config store`,`MySQL`,`Redis`,`Background jobs`,`Rate limiting`,`Load shedding`,`Chaos engineering`],note:`The request path meets them in order: a global router maps each store to its pod, the pod runs the full Rails monolith with its own MySQL and Redis, and per-store rate limits, async queues, and load-shedding tiers absorb the spike inside that cell.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why does one store's spike become everyone's problem?`,body:[`Here's the question I'd ask if I walked into your team the quarter before Black Friday: what's the biggest thing one tenant can break? Shopify is a multi-tenant SaaS — hundreds of thousands of storefronts on one platform — and the risk shape is nasty: any store can flash-sale at any minute. A celebrity drop, a viral product, a coordinated marketing push. You cannot predict which store, which day, which hour. If tenants share capacity naively, one spike degrades everyone, and your worst outage is caused by your most successful customer.`,`The instinctive fix is 'add capacity' — autoscale the whole platform and ride it out. Why doesn't that work? First, the spike is single-tenant, so platform-wide headroom means the many permanently pay to cover the one. Second, and worse, it doesn't touch the real failure mode: contention on shared state — inventory rows, checkout locks, connection pools. Throwing boxes at contention gets you a bigger queue, not a solution. The answer has to be structural: bound the blast radius so a spike physically cannot reach other tenants.`],quote:`Blast-radius limits are the only multi-tenant feature your customers will never know exists — and never forgive you for missing.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Global autoscale, shared capacity`,`One fleet, simple ops`,`One spike degrades all tenants; contention on shared state remains`,`Poor`],[`Tenant-to-cell pods`,`Blast radius = one cell; hot path stays local`,`Needs routing and resharding tooling`,`Excellent - Shopify's choice`],[`Microservices per function`,`Independent scaling per service`,`Tenant contention spreads across services and network hops`,`Poor for tenant spikes`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Shopify — Flash sales`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-shopify-flash.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what a pod actually is`,body:[`So what is a pod, concretely? A complete vertical stack: the same Rails monolith codebase running everywhere, with its own MySQL primary, its own Redis, its own background-job workers. From the application's point of view, the pod is the world — the code never queries across pods. Stores inside a pod are co-tenants exactly the way they'd be on one shared database, just with walls around the whole building.`,`The only cross-pod operation in the hot path is the very first hop: a request router resolves shop.example.com to pod-42 using a mapping held in a small, highly-replicated config store. One lookup, then the request lives and dies inside the pod for its entire lifecycle. That's the whole trick — one cheap replicated read at the edge buys you a fully local hot path everywhere else.`,`And why not microservices? Because a flash sale's failure mode is contention over shared state — inventory, checkout — and splitting by function scatters that contention across services and network hops. A spike that used to be one pod's problem becomes a platform-wide thundering herd. The monolith-in-a-cell keeps the blast radius physical instead of logical, and debugging stays boring: one codebase, one stack, one place to look.`],quote:`Partition by tenant, not by function — the spike should die in the cell where it was born.`,table:{headers:[`Design choice`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Monolith per pod`,`Local hot path, one deploy unit, simple debugging`,`Requires discipline in a huge codebase`,`Proven at 533k RPS`],[`Microservices per function`,`Independent deploys and scaling`,`Tenant contention crosses network hops; distributed debugging`,`Poor fit for flash-sale contention`],[`Shared services across pods`,`Saves capacity`,`Every spike becomes a platform event`,`Anti-pattern here`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Shopify — Flash sales`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-shopify-flash.html`}},{heading:`03 · Inside the cell: throttle, queue, shed`,body:[`Isolation between pods isn't enough — you need isolation inside the pod too, because a pod holds many stores. So each store gets per-store rate limits, Stripe-style: a hard cap on what any single tenant can consume of the pod's capacity. The cell bounds the blast radius; the limiter bounds the tenant. Two rings, and the spike has nowhere left to spread.`,`Next: everything that isn't time-critical goes asynchronous. Webhook deliveries, fulfillment notifications, marketing emails, analytics events — all background jobs. During the spike the queues grow; consumers drain them after. The shopper sees a fast checkout; the downstream effects land within minutes. That's the discipline worth stealing: keep the synchronous path small enough that you can name every request in it from memory.`,`And when the pod itself saturates, you don't improvise — you execute a pre-written degradation ladder. Recommendations turn off. Real-time inventory previews fall back to stale-but-cached. Search routes to a degraded mode. Checkout is the last thing standing. Shopify rehearses this with chaos engineering targeted at individual pods: break things on purpose, verify the rest of the platform keeps running. The worst case is a drill, not a discovery.`],quote:`Checkout is the last thing standing because it's the only thing that makes money.`,table:{headers:[`Degradation tier`,`What gives way`,`Shopper impact`],rows:[[`Tier 1`,`Recommendations off`,`None - cosmetic`],[`Tier 2`,`Live inventory preview falls back to stale cache`,`Minor - counts may lag`],[`Tier 3`,`Search routes to degraded mode`,`Moderate - discovery slows`],[`Last`,`Checkout`,`Never shedded while money flows`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Shopify — Flash sales`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-shopify-flash.html`}},{heading:`04 · The unglamorous half: resharding keeps isolation honest`,body:[`Here's the part nobody puts on a conference slide: isolation decays. Pods drift hot — a few large merchants land together, their traffic grows, and your 'small blast radius' quietly becomes a big one. Shopify's answer is that resharding is a routine, automated operation: move merchants from hot pods to cooler ones during low-traffic windows. The merchant doesn't notice, except in the rare move that hits a bug.`,`Zoom out one level and pods are grouped into regions — cell-based architecture. That second ring is what makes the failure drills meaningful: you can lose a pod and keep serving, lose a region and keep serving, and chaos experiments target one pod precisely because the blast radius is designed to stop there. Capacity planning gets a natural unit too: you scale by adding pods, not by stretching a shared everything.`,`This is the real lesson of the 32M RPM Black Friday. The architecture works because moving tenants is boring. If resharding were a quarterly crisis, pods would bloat until 'isolation' was a diagram, not a property. Sustained isolation is an operational practice, not a one-time design decision.`],quote:`Isolation you can't rebalance is just a prettier single point of failure.`,table:{headers:[`Pod sizing lever`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Fewer, bigger pods`,`Less routing state, fewer moves`,`Bigger blast radius per failure`,`Tempting, wrong`],[`More, smaller pods`,`Tiny blast radius, clean chaos drills`,`More resharding churn and routing state`,`Shopify's direction`],[`Static pods, no resharding`,`Zero migration tooling`,`Hot pods accumulate; isolation decays`,`Fails within quarters`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Shopify — Flash sales`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-shopify-flash.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`pod-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One lookup at the edge, then everything is local`,caption:`The router's store-to-pod lookup is the only cross-pod operation in the hot path; the flash-sale pod contains the entire spike — monolith, MySQL, Redis, queues, workers — while every other pod's load stays flat.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    Traffic["Shopper traffic<br/>peak ~533k RPS = 32M RPM"] --> Router["Global request router"]
    Router -.->|"one replicated lookup"| Map[("Config store<br/>store-to-pod map")]
    Router --> PodA["Pod A<br/>full vertical stack"]
    Router --> PodC["Pod C<br/>full vertical stack"]
    subgraph PodB["Pod B - flash-sale pod"]
        direction TB
        Limit["Per-store rate limiter"] --> Mono["Rails monolith<br/>synchronous checkout path"]
        Mono --> MySQL[("Pod-local MySQL")]
        Mono --> Redis[("Pod-local Redis")]
        Mono --> Queue[("Async queues<br/>webhooks, emails, analytics")]
        Queue --> Workers["Background workers<br/>drain after the spike"]
    end
    Router --> Limit`},{id:`flash-sale-request`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Anatomy of a flash-sale request`,caption:`Synchronous work is checkout-only; everything else is queued and drained after the spike, and the degradation ladder fires in order if the pod saturates.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant U as Shopper
    participant R as Request router
    participant P as Pod - Rails monolith
    participant Q as Async queues
    U->>R: browse and checkout
    R->>R: resolve store to pod
    R->>P: forward into pod
    P->>P: per-store rate-limit check
    P->>P: reserve inventory, charge card
    P-->>U: order confirmed
    P->>Q: enqueue webhook, email, analytics
    Note over Q: queue depth grows during the spike
    Q->>Q: workers drain after the spike
    Note over P,Q: if the pod saturates - shed recommendations,<br/>serve stale inventory cache, degrade search,<br/>checkout is the last to fall`}],takeaways:[`Choose a unit of isolation you can afford to lose: Shopify's pod is a complete stack per shard, so a viral merchant's blast radius is one pod — never the platform-wide 533k RPS.`,`Budget exactly one cross-cell operation in the hot path — a single replicated store-to-pod lookup at the router. If a request crosses a second boundary, redesign before you scale.`,`Cap every tenant with per-store rate limits so the worst tenant can only take a bounded slice of its own cell; isolation must exist inside the cell, not just between cells.`,`Keep the synchronous path small enough to recite from memory — checkout only. Webhooks, emails, fulfillment, and analytics ride queues that grow during the spike and drain within minutes after.`,`Write the degradation ladder before the spike — recommendations off, stale inventory cache, degraded search, checkout last — and rehearse it with pod-scoped chaos drills on a schedule, not during the incident.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Shopify — Flash sales`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-shopify-flash.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`case-slack`,pathId:`case-studies`,order:11,title:`Slack architecture`,subtitle:`Slack's architectural story is unusual: PHP. Sharded by workspace. The architecture was built for a particular shape — small-to-mid-size teams in their own private universe — and h`,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[],story:{headline:`The Monday the presence graph ate the fleet.`,paragraphs:[`It's Monday, 9:07 AM. A five-figure-seat enterprise workspace finished its migration over the weekend, and the entire company opens Slack at once. Within minutes, typing indicators stall, presence dots spin gray, and messages that used to land in milliseconds start arriving in seconds. The pager goes off.`,`The on-call trace is almost funny in hindsight. The data tier is healthy. The message path is healthy. What's drowning is the fan-out: every presence flip — online, away, back online — was being broadcast to every member of the workspace. With tens of thousands of members, each status change fans out tens of thousands of deliveries, and on a Monday morning everyone flips status at once. That's O(N²) traffic with N in the tens of thousands, and the WebSocket edge is saturated by green dots, not messages.`,`The fix wasn't more flannel boxes. It was a redesign: lazy presence — stop telling everyone everything, and compute presence only for the handful of people a user is actually looking at. That incident is the whole Slack architecture in miniature: the workspace is the universe, nothing crosses it, and every design decision optimizes for that shape.`],problem:`How do you serve real-time chat for a 5-person startup and a 50,000-person enterprise on the same architecture — without pretending to be one global system?`},tech:{stack:[`PHP`,`Workspace Sharding`,`WebSockets`,`flannel`,`Solr`,`SQS / Kinesis`,`Channel Pub-Sub`,`Lazy Presence`],note:`PHP services per workspace shard, flannel terminating the WebSocket fleet, SQS/Kinesis between services, and a Solr collection per workspace — every hot-path component lives inside one workspace's universe.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why not just build one big global system?`,body:[`Okay, first question I get every time: why not one big global system? You hear 'chat at Slack scale' and the instinct is a planet-sized message bus, a global search cluster, one giant everything. But stop and look at the actual data shape. A user inside Acme's workspace never touches a byte of Globex's workspace. No cross-workspace search. No cross-workspace anything on the hot path.`,`So Slack partitioned by workspace, end-to-end — the same cell pattern Shopify uses. Every workspace lives on a single shard: its messages, its search index, its presence. Small workspaces share machines; a 100k-user workspace gets its own capacity and grows without touching the 50-person workspace next door. Blast radius is one workspace. When a shard misbehaves, exactly one tenant universe wobbles.`,`And the discipline is the part people miss: the moment you add one cross-workspace query 'just for this feature,' you've rebuilt the global system with extra steps. Slack held the line — no cross-workspace queries on the hot path, period.`],quote:`Per-tenant is the unit of fail. Anything bigger than one tenant is over-scoped.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`One global system`,`Single source of truth, simple mental model`,`Every query is cross-tenant, blast radius is everything`,`Poor`],[`Workspace shards`,`Blast radius = one workspace, capacity sized per shard`,`Shard placement and rebalancing work`,`Excellent`],[`Hybrid: global metadata, sharded data`,`Enables cross-workspace features`,`Two consistency domains on the hot path`,`Situational`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Slack architecture`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-slack.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the real-time path: who actually pushes the message?`,body:[`Now walk a message with me. Alice hits send. The message persists to her workspace's shard, and the channel — every channel is a pub/sub topic internally — gets a publish. The topic layer looks up subscribers, and flannel, Slack's homegrown WebSocket proxy fleet, fans the message out to every connected client.`,`Here's the design decision I want you to sit with: the data tier never pushes to clients. flannel terminates the WebSockets, caches what each client needs, and owns delivery. Why does that matter? Because if your database pushes, your database holds connection state — and every reconnect storm, every mobile client coming back from a tunnel, hammers your storage tier. With flannel, the edge scales independently of the data tier, and a workspace's connections can spike without its storage moving at all.`],quote:`The data tier stores; the edge delivers. Never let your database hold a socket.`,table:{headers:[`Delivery design`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Data tier pushes to clients`,`No extra tier to run`,`Storage holds sockets, reconnect storms hit the DB`,`Poor`],[`Dedicated WS proxy tier (flannel)`,`Edge scales independently, caches per-client needs`,`Another fleet to operate`,`Excellent`],[`Client polling`,`Dead simple`,`Latency plus fan-out cost on every poll`,`Never`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Slack architecture`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-slack.html`}},{heading:`03 · The presence trap: the O(N²) that ate Monday`,body:[`Presence looks like the simplest feature in the product — a little green dot — and it's the one that ate a Monday morning. The naive design: keep a per-user TTL'd record in a presence cache, and when my status flips, broadcast it to every member of my workspace. At fifty people? Free. At fifty thousand? Every flip is fifty thousand deliveries, everyone flips at 9 AM, and you're generating O(N²) traffic before anyone has typed a word.`,`The redesign is the lesson: lazy presence. Stop telling everyone everything. When you open a DM list or a channel header, that's the moment presence gets computed — for the handful of users actually on your screen. O(viewport) instead of O(N²). The cache and the TTLs stay; the broadcast dies.`],quote:`Broadcast state scales O(N²). Lazy state scales O(screen).`,table:{headers:[`Presence design`,`Cost per status flip`,`Fails at`],rows:[[`Broadcast to all members`,`O(N) per flip, O(N²) across a morning`,`Five-figure workspaces`],[`Lazy, per-viewer lookup`,`O(viewers on screen)`,`Effectively never`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Slack architecture`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-slack.html`}},{heading:`04 · Search: your workspace is your search universe`,body:[`Last path: search. Each workspace gets its own Solr collection — the whole message history of that workspace, and nothing else. When you search, your query scopes to your workspace's index. That's why Slack search feels 'fast for me': your search universe is your workspace, not the entire product. No global index to shard, no cross-tenant result filtering.`,`And notice the pipeline shape: message lands, gets published to a queue, and an indexer writes it into Solr asynchronously. A few seconds of 'not yet searchable' is completely acceptable — instant message delivery is not. Those are two different SLAs, so they're two different paths. If you coupled them, every send would block on a search cluster write, and your search capacity would cap your messaging throughput.`],quote:`Instant delivery and instant searchability are different SLAs. Never couple them.`,table:{headers:[`Indexing path`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Synchronous index on write`,`Searchable immediately`,`Write path blocks on the search cluster`,`Poor`],[`Async queue into Solr`,`Delivery never waits on search`,`Seconds of search lag`,`Excellent`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Slack architecture`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-slack.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`slack-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Slack workspace-sharded topology`,caption:`One workspace, one shard, end-to-end: storage, pub/sub, presence, and search all live inside the same universe, while flannel owns the WebSocket edge.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  subgraph clients[Clients]
    C[Browser and mobile apps]
  end
  subgraph edge[Real-time edge tier]
    FL[flannel WebSocket proxy fleet]
  end
  subgraph shard[One workspace shard]
    API[PHP app services]
    DB[Shard datastore]
    TOPIC[Channel pub-sub topics]
    PRES[Presence cache with TTLs]
  end
  subgraph pipes[Async pipelines]
    Q[SQS and Kinesis queues]
    IDX[Indexer workers]
    SOLR[Per-workspace Solr index]
  end
  C -->|WebSocket session| FL
  FL -->|fan-out| C
  FL -->|subscribe| TOPIC
  TOPIC -->|publish| FL
  API --> DB
  API --> TOPIC
  API -->|presence TTLs| PRES
  API -->|message events| Q
  Q --> IDX
  IDX --> SOLR
  C -->|search query| API
  API -->|scoped query| SOLR`},{id:`message-and-presence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Message send and lazy presence`,caption:`The hot path: persist, publish to the channel topic, fan out via flannel — while indexing and presence stay off the critical path.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant A as Alice
  participant API as Shard API
  participant T as Channel topic
  participant F as flannel
  participant B as Bob
  participant S as Solr
  A->>API: send message
  API->>API: persist to shard datastore
  API->>T: publish to channel topic
  T->>F: notify subscribers
  F->>B: push over WebSocket
  API->>API: enqueue index event
  Note over API,S: async path - seconds of lag is fine
  API->>S: index message
  B->>API: open DM list with Alice
  API->>API: lazy presence lookup for Alice only
  API-->>B: presence badge`}],takeaways:[`Partition by tenant end-to-end: blast radius = one workspace, capacity sized per shard, and zero cross-tenant queries on the hot path.`,`Never let your data tier hold client sockets — terminate WebSockets in a dedicated proxy tier (flannel) so edges and storage scale independently.`,`Broadcast state scales O(N²); lazy state scales O(screen). Compute presence only for users someone is actually looking at.`,`Decouple delivery from searchability: instant WebSocket push, seconds-late async Solr indexing — two paths, two SLAs.`,`Boring tech with the right partition shape beats exotic global systems: PHP and Solr carried chat from teams of 5 to five-figure enterprises.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Slack architecture`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-slack.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`case-stripe-rate-limiting`,pathId:`case-studies`,order:12,title:`Stripe — Four rate limiters`,subtitle:`Stripe runs four different rate-limiters on every incoming API call. Each protects against a different failure mode. The public blog post from 2017 is the most-cited rate-limiting `,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[`4 distinct limiters`,`Redis + Lua`],story:{headline:`The limiter that saved Black Friday was the one we almost deleted.`,paragraphs:[`2:47 AM, the Friday before Black Friday. Pager: p99 latency on POST /v1/charges is climbing fast. I open the per-user rate dashboard first — muscle memory — and it's a wall of green. Every tenant comfortably under their request-rate limit. For ninety seconds I believed we were fine. We were not fine: the charge workers' thread pools were saturated, and every merchant on the platform was paying for it.`,`The culprit: one large merchant's reconciliation job. Their downstream payment processor had degraded, so every call held a worker for ~30 seconds — and they kept 40 in flight, all night. Requests-per-second? Textbook normal. Concurrency? Through the roof. Limiter #2, the per-user concurrency cap, was the only one that could even see the problem. It tripped, shed their load, and the fleet exhaled.`,`The postmortem slide that stuck with me: 'Every limiter is blind to something. We run four because we've been burned at least four different ways.' Stripe's 2017 write-up became the most-cited rate-limiting design in the industry — not because the numbers are magic, but because it's the rare public doc that admits one number was never going to be enough.`],problem:`Why does a single rate limit — even a well-tuned one — fail to protect a payments fleet, and what exactly has to be counted on every request?`},tech:{stack:[`Redis`,`Lua scripts`,`Token bucket`,`Concurrency caps`,`Fleet-wide counter`,`Endpoint backstop`],note:`Redis with atomic Lua scripts sits in the API-gateway hot path, evaluating four independent limiters per request — per-user rate, per-user concurrency, fleet-wide total, per-endpoint backstop — at sub-millisecond p99.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why the tutorial limiter melts at scale`,body:[`Here's the version every tutorial hands you: one token bucket per user, INCR in Redis, compare against 100, allow or deny. It works great right up until you're the payment rail for the internet. At Stripe scale it misses three failure modes, and each one pages a different team.`,`Failure mode one: the slow request. A user sits comfortably under their RPS limit while every call holds a worker for 30 seconds — ten of those in flight and they've monopolized ten threads the RPS counter never saw. Failure mode two: the synchronized burst. Ten thousand well-behaved users at 99 RPS each is roughly a million RPS against your fleet, and no per-user counter flinches. Failure mode three: cost asymmetry. GET /customers costs about a millisecond of CPU; POST /charges costs ~200ms, touches a payment network, and spends partner credit. '100 RPS' of one is a Tuesday; '100 RPS' of the other is an incident.`,`So the real question was never 'should we rate limit?' It was 'what resource are we protecting on this request?' — and the honest answer is four different resources.`],quote:`A rate limiter doesn't limit requests — it limits a resource. Name the resource first, or your limiter is decoration.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Catches`,`Misses`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Single per-user RPS`,`One runaway tenant`,`Slow requests, fleet bursts, costly endpoints`,`Toy scale`],[`Per-user + fleet-wide`,`Runaway tenant + aggregate overload`,`Concurrency, endpoint cost`,`Closer, still blind`],[`All four limiters`,`Every failure mode independently`,`Only cost: four checks per request`,`Payments scale`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Stripe — Four rate limiters`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-stripe-rate-limiting.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: four limiters, four resources`,body:[`So we walk the boundary. Limiter one, per-user request rate — that's customer fairness; it catches the runaway integration looping POSTs at 3 AM. Limiter two, per-user concurrency — that's downstream concurrency; it counts in-flight requests, not arrivals: increment on entry, decrement on completion, and you need a TTL safety net because a crashed connection will otherwise leak a slot forever. Limiter three, fleet-wide overall — total fleet capacity; it's the only thing that notices when a feature launch makes ten thousand integrations poll at once. Limiter four, per-endpoint backstop — hot-route protection; it prices the expensive path independently of everything else.`,`Here's the part people miss: these aren't stages in a pipeline. They're four independent vetoes. Any one of them can refuse the request, and the request doesn't need a majority — it needs all four to say yes. That's deliberate: a veto you can override by 'passing the others' isn't a veto.`,`Why not merge them into one clever score? Because their counters live at different scopes — user, user-in-flight, fleet, endpoint — and different time constants. Merge them and one knob now tunes four resources; you'll calibrate it during the loudest incident and silently break the protection the quiet ones depended on.`],quote:`The request doesn't need a majority — it needs all four limiters to say yes.`,table:{headers:[`Limiter`,`Protects`,`Catches`,`Blind to`],rows:[[`1 · Per-user request rate`,`Customer fairness`,`Runaway retry loop at 3 AM`,`Slow requests, aggregate load`],[`2 · Per-user concurrency`,`Downstream concurrency`,`30-second requests stacking up`,`Arrival rate`],[`3 · Fleet-wide overall`,`Total fleet capacity`,`10k users × 99 RPS each`,`Individual fairness`],[`4 · Per-endpoint backstop`,`Hot, expensive routes`,`100 RPS of POST /charges`,`Cross-endpoint totals`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Stripe — Four rate limiters`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-stripe-rate-limiting.html`}},{heading:`03 · Why Redis + Lua — and what happens when Redis blinks`,body:[`Now the implementation question I get most in Q&A: why Redis, and why Lua? Because the check has to be atomic. Read the counter, compare, increment, set the expiry — if those are four round trips from the gateway, two gateway nodes can race the same user and both allow. The Lua script runs the entire decision inside Redis, single-threaded, indivisible. One EVAL, one answer.`,`And it has to be fast in a way nothing else in the stack does: this check runs on every single request, so it must be cheaper than the work it protects. Sub-millisecond p99 on the limit check is the whole design constraint — that's why it's Redis and not Postgres, and a script and not a rules engine.`,`The uncomfortable follow-up: what happens when Redis blinks? Fail open — keep serving, lose protection — or fail closed — keep protection, reject everything? The blog post won't decide for you. Decide in advance, in writing, with your incident lead in the room. That decision made at 3 AM is always the wrong one.`],quote:`If your limit check can be raced, it isn't a rate limit — it's a suggestion.`,table:{headers:[`Redis outage choice`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Fail open`,`API stays available`,`Fleet unprotected mid-incident`,`Low-risk read APIs`],[`Fail closed`,`Protection always holds`,`Redis outage becomes API outage`,`Expensive writes`],[`Fail open + local fallback`,`Bounded protection, stays up`,`Approximate counts, more code`,`High-scale gateways`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Stripe — Four rate limiters`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-stripe-rate-limiting.html`}},{heading:`04 · The 429 that explains itself`,body:[`Last thing, and it's the part customers actually touch: the 429. The 2017 post is the most-cited rate-limiting design in the industry, and I think it's partly because the numbers are honest — calibrated for cardholders, meaning tuned against the worst real week on the platform, not the median Tuesday in a demo app.`,`When a limit trips, the response says which one. 'You hit your concurrency limit, retry after one second' is a config change on the customer's side. 'You hit your rate limit' is a support ticket. Same rejection, wildly different cost to both companies.`,`Four limiters sounds like over-engineering until you price a request properly: cost varies by 100× across endpoints, and the cost of an outage is measured in news coverage. Then four is the minimum.`],quote:`You can't talk a customer out of a 429. You can give them a header that tells them which 429 they got.`,table:{headers:[`Signal in the 429`,`What the customer learns`,`Ticket it prevents`],rows:[[`Retry-After: 1s`,`Exactly when to come back`,`The 'when can we retry?' email`],[`limit: user_concurrency`,`It is in-flight count, not RPS`,`The 'we are under our limit!' ticket`],[`limit: fleet_capacity`,`Everyone is throttled, not just you`,`The 'are we banned?' escalation`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Stripe — Four rate limiters`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-stripe-rate-limiting.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`four-vetoes-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Four independent vetoes on every request`,caption:`Every API call is evaluated against all four limiters; any single refusal ends the request with a 429 that names the limiter.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  M["Merchant integrations"] --> GW["Global API gateway"]
  subgraph V["Four independent vetoes"]
    L1["1 · Per-user request rate"]
    L2["2 · Per-user concurrency"]
    L3["3 · Fleet-wide overall"]
    L4["4 · Per-endpoint backstop"]
  end
  GW --> L1
  GW --> L2
  GW --> L3
  GW --> L4
  L1 --> R[("Redis + Lua")]
  L2 --> R
  L3 --> R
  L4 --> R
  GW -->|all four pass| S["Core payment services"]
  GW -->|any one refuses| X["429 + which limiter tripped"]
  X -.->|Retry-After| M`},{id:`concurrency-trip-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Anatomy of a concurrency 429`,caption:`The RPS counter stays green while the concurrency cap trips — the customer gets a named limit and a Retry-After instead of starting a retry storm.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant C as Merchant integration
  participant G as API gateway
  participant R as Redis + Lua
  participant P as Payment processor
  C->>G: POST /v1/charges
  G->>R: EVAL check_limits(user, endpoint)
  R-->>G: rate OK · concurrency FULL
  G-->>C: 429 · Retry-After: 1s · limit: user_concurrency
  Note over C: Backs off with jitter — no retry storm
  C->>G: POST /v1/charges after backoff
  G->>R: EVAL check_limits(user, endpoint)
  R-->>G: all four pass
  G->>P: Forward charge
  P-->>G: authorized
  G-->>C: 201 Created`}],takeaways:[`Run four limiters per request — per-user rate, per-user concurrency, fleet-wide total, per-endpoint backstop — one limiter is too coarse to be useful at scale.`,`Keep the limit check under 1ms p99 (atomic Redis + Lua): a limiter that adds 20ms to every call costs more than the incident it prevents.`,`Concurrency is a separate budget: ten in-flight 30-second requests hold ten threads no matter what the RPS counter says.`,`Price endpoints separately: a ~1ms read and a ~200ms payment-network write differ by 100× — never share one '100 RPS' number between them.`,`Ship a 429 that names the limiter and sets Retry-After — you can't talk a customer out of a 429, but you can make it actionable.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Stripe — Four rate limiters`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-stripe-rate-limiting.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`case-tinder`,pathId:`case-studies`,order:13,title:`Tinder — 1.6B swipes/day`,subtitle:`Tinder's swipe gesture is the world's most-used UI mechanism. Underneath: a geo-indexed candidate pool, a match-detection engine that fires on mutual right-swipes, and a write-heav`,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[],story:{headline:`Sunday, 9:04 PM: the matches went quiet.`,paragraphs:[`Sunday night is the swipe super-bowl. At 9:04 PM, writes per second hit triple the daily mean, and the on-call pager lights up: match notifications are landing three to six minutes late. Users right-swipe, put the phone down, and the "It's a match!" push arrives after the screen is already locked. Within the hour, conversation-start rate on brand-new matches drops double digits.`,`The trail leads to the match detector. Every right-swipe asks exactly one question: has this person already right-swiped me? That answer lives in a denormalized "liked-by" index — a second write fanned out on every right-swipe so the match check is a local read instead of a cross-shard trip. Under peak load, the fan-out writer fell behind, the index went stale, and match checks queued behind it.`,`The fix wasn't heroic: backpressure on the fan-out, a freshness SLA on the index, and a hard rule that a match push is a seconds-level event, not a minutes-level one. The lesson stuck permanently: at this scale the swipe is the cheap part — the moment of reciprocity is the product, and it decays by the minute.`],problem:`A peak-hour swipe storm backed up the denormalized liked-by index; match detection lagged and push notifications landed minutes late — past the window where users still care.`},tech:{stack:[`Elasticsearch`,`Per-user Sharding`,`Denormalized Liked-by Index`,`APNs / FCM`,`TTL'd Swipe Store`],note:`Elasticsearch serves the geo-filtered, ML-ranked candidate deck; per-user sharded stores absorb the write-heavy swipe firehose; a denormalized liked-by index turns cross-shard match checks into local reads; APNs/FCM deliver the seconds-level match push that converts reciprocity into a conversation.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: nobody swipes on infrastructure`,body:[`Let's start where the user starts: the deck. What looks like a card is actually a recommendation query — geo-filter by location, filter age range and gender preferences, then rank by a model blending activity recency with learned attractiveness signals. Elasticsearch is the candidate index here; it returns the top 50, and the client pre-fetches the next batch while you're still swiping the current one.`,`Why not cache the deck aggressively? Because the deck is the product. A stale deck means showing people who've already been swiped, left the app, or changed photos — and every dead card trains the user to stop opening the app. So we spend real latency budget on ranking and hide it behind the gesture: by the time you've decided on card 12, cards 13 through 50 are already on your phone.`],quote:`The hard problem isn't the swipe. It's making sure the next face is worth swiping on.`,table:{headers:[`Rank signal`,`Compute cost`,`Freshness window`],rows:[[`Geo + age + gender filters`,`Cheap — indexed`,`Minutes`],[`Activity recency`,`Cheap`,`Hours`],[`ML attractiveness score`,`Expensive — model inference`,`Days`],[`Cached deck (anti-pattern)`,`Free`,`Stale in minutes`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Tinder — 1.6B swipes/day`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-tinder.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the write path: 20k tiny writes a second`,body:[`Now the firehose. 1.6 billion swipes a day works out to roughly 18.5k writes per second on average — and Sunday at 9 PM it's more like 80k. Each swipe is a tiny tuple: user_id, target_id, direction, timestamp. That's it. No joins, no edits, no transactions spanning users.`,`So why not one big SQL table with secondary indexes? Look at the read pattern: a swipe is nearly write-only. Months-old swipes barely matter — the person may be inactive, may have new photos, may have deleted the app. So we partition per user (one user's swipes live on one shard, writes stay local and append-cheap) and put a TTL on cold swipes to keep the working set bounded. Treat it like a log, not a ledger.`],quote:`A swipe is a write with a near-zero read half-life — log it, don't ledger it.`,table:{headers:[`Design choice`,`Why it wins`,`What it costs`],rows:[[`Per-user sharding`,`Local appends, no cross-shard write contention`,`Popular users skew shard load`],[`Append-only tuples`,`Cheap at 80k writes/sec`,`No in-place edits or corrections`],[`TTL on cold swipes`,`Bounded dataset, fast lookups`,`An ancient like can never match`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Tinder — 1.6B swipes/day`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-tinder.html`}},{heading:`03 · The cross-shard question: did Bob already like me?`,body:[`Here's the asymmetry that bites everyone. Alice right-swipes Bob — a match exists only if Bob already right-swiped Alice. But Bob's swipe history lives on Bob's shard. The one question that matters on the hot path is a cross-shard read.`,`Why not just eat the cross-shard read? You could — but you can't know the read is rare until you've done it, and at peak that's 80k shard-to-shard lookups a second with p99s piling up. The fix is denormalization: every right-swipe writes twice. Alice's shard gets "I liked Bob"; Bob's liked-by index gets "Alice liked me." Now the match check is one local read, single-digit milliseconds. Two writes per right-swipe, one read per match check — write amplification traded for read locality.`,`And this is exactly where our 3 AM incident lived: the fan-out writer fell behind at peak, the liked-by index went stale, and match checks queued behind it. Denormalization isn't free — it's a consistency contract you have to SLA.`],quote:`When a read crosses a shard boundary, buy it with a second write — then SLA that write.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Writes per right-swipe`,`Match check cost`,`Failure mode`],rows:[[`Normalized — single source of truth`,`1`,`Cross-shard read, p99 queues at peak`,`Match detector couples to every shard`],[`Denormalized liked-by index`,`2`,`Local read, single-digit ms`,`Staleness — needs freshness SLA`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Tinder — 1.6B swipes/day`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-tinder.html`}},{heading:`04 · Why not batch the pushes? Because seconds are the product`,body:[`Match detected. Now the part engineers undervalue: the push. Both devices get notified via APNs or FCM — and the target is seconds, not minutes. Why so religious about it? Because the user is literally holding the phone mid-session. A push that lands now opens a chat; a push that lands in an hour lands on a locked screen and dies in a notification tray.`,`Why not batch pushes to be gentle on APNs/FCM quotas? Batching saves pennies and costs conversations. The notification is the conversion event — it's the moment two strangers become a thread. Our incident proved it empirically: when pushes slipped from seconds to minutes, new-match conversation starts dropped double digits within the hour.`],quote:`A match is worth nothing until both people know about it — and 'both' has a shelf life in seconds.`,table:{headers:[`Delivery window`,`User state`,`Outcome`],rows:[[`0–30 seconds`,`In-session, phone in hand`,`Chat opens immediately`],[`2–10 minutes`,`Screen locked`,`Push buried, session lost`],[`1 hour+`,`App abandoned`,`Match found cold, low reply rate`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Tinder — 1.6B swipes/day`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-tinder.html`}},{heading:`05 · The 1% rule: spend where reciprocity happens`,body:[`Zoom out and the economics are almost funny. Almost every swipe is a no-match. The expensive lookup — "did the other side already like me?" — fires once per match, which is under 1% of swipes. The other 99% is a dumb append we've made as cheap as physically possible.`,`So where do you spend engineering effort? Not shaving microseconds off the swipe write — it's already a tiny append; the marginal win is noise. The leverage is all in the rare branch: index freshness, match-detection latency, push delivery. Optimize the 99% for cost, the 1% for delight. That's the whole architecture in one sentence.`],quote:`Optimize the 99% for cost, the 1% for delight.`,source:{label:`sharpoverride — Tinder — 1.6B swipes/day`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-tinder.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`swipe-match-pipeline`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Swipe-to-match pipeline`,caption:`The full loop: discovery feeds the deck, the swipe firehose fans out into two writes, and match detection is one local read away from a push.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  C["Mobile Client"] --> D["Deck Service"]
  D --> ES["Elasticsearch Geo Candidate Index"]
  C --> S["Swipe API"]
  S --> W1["Swipe Shard — user partitioned"]
  S --> W2["Liked-by Index — denormalized fan-out"]
  S --> M["Match Detector"]
  M -->|local read| W2
  M --> MS["Match Store"]
  MS --> P["Push Service — APNs / FCM"]
  P --> U["Both Devices"]`},{id:`match-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Alice right-swipes Bob; Bob already liked her`,caption:`The happy path in five hops: two writes, one local read, one match row, two pushes — all inside a few seconds.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  participant A as Alice Client
  participant API as Swipe API
  participant S as Swipe Shard Alice
  participant L as Liked-by Index Bob
  participant M as Match Detector
  participant P as Push Service
  participant B as Bob Client

  A->>API: right-swipe Bob
  API->>S: append Alice, Bob, right, ts
  API->>L: fan-out write — Bob liked-by Alice
  API->>M: evaluate match Alice-Bob
  M->>L: did Bob right-swipe Alice?
  L-->>M: yes — local read, single-digit ms
  M->>M: create match row
  M->>P: notify both
  P-->>A: match push — within seconds
  P-->>B: match push — within seconds`}],takeaways:[`1.6B swipes/day ≈ 18.5k writes/sec average and ~80k at peak — capacity-plan for the Sunday-night peak, not the daily mean.`,`Under 1% of swipes ever match — keep the 99% path a cheap append-only write and spend your latency budget on the 1% that reciprocates.`,`When a match check crosses a shard boundary, denormalize: two writes per right-swipe buy one local read per match check.`,`Deliver match pushes in seconds, not minutes — the notification is the conversion event, and it decays by the minute.`,`TTL cold swipes: months-old likes are dead signal; bounding the swipe dataset keeps shards small and match checks fast.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Tinder — 1.6B swipes/day`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-tinder.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`},{kind:`youtube`,label:`Hello Interview — System Design Interview: Design Tinder w/ a Ex-Meta Staff Engineer`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=18Fg5Akhkqw`,author:`Hello Interview`,note:`Designing Tinder, live.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`case-uber-nearby`,pathId:`case-studies`,order:14,title:`Uber — Nearby drivers`,subtitle:`Every rider's phone polls "show me nearby drivers" every 4 seconds. With millions of riders, that's 1M+ spatial queries per second against millions of moving driver positions. Uber`,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[],story:{headline:`The night the map went blank over Times Square`,paragraphs:[`11:47 PM, December 31st. Times Square. The rider app shows a spinner, then the words every product manager dreads: 'No drivers nearby.' Meanwhile the street below is a solid ribbon of headlights — millions of drivers online, thousands within a kilometer of that pin. Support tickets are stacking, p99 on the geo service is at nine seconds and climbing, and I'm the on-call with a laptop and a deadline that is literally midnight.`,`The root cause was embarrassingly arithmetic. Every rider polls 'show me nearby drivers' every 4 seconds; every driver pushes a GPS position every 4 seconds. At New Year's density that's a million-plus spatial queries per second layered over millions of moving writes — and every one of them was a radius scan in Postgres against an indexed lat/lon column. The B-tree was being rewritten faster than anyone could walk it. The database wasn't broken; it was being asked to do live geometry at a rate live geometry was never designed for.`,`We limped through the night on a band-aid — coarser grid, aggressive caching, shedding polls — and spent the following week asking a better question. Not 'how do we make this query faster?' but 'what shape makes this query cheap by construction?' The answer was hexagons. Uber asked the same question, answered it with H3, and open-sourced the whole thing.`],problem:`Serve 'drivers near me' in milliseconds at 1M+ queries per second while millions of driver positions churn every 4 seconds.`},tech:{stack:[`H3 Hex Grid`,`In-Memory Cell Sets`,`Redis`,`k-Ring Lookup`,`Prefix Sharding`,`Adaptive Resolution`],note:`Driver positions live in in-memory sets keyed by H3 cell ID (Redis in Uber's early build); a thin geo service shards cells by hex prefix and answers each nearby query with a k-ring of hash lookups.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why not just ask Postgres?`,body:[`Start where every prototype starts: SELECT * FROM drivers WHERE earth_distance(point, rider_point) < 2000. With fifty drivers in dev, that's a one-liner and you go to lunch. Now do the production math out loud: a million riders polling every 4 seconds is ~250k reads per second, and 7M drivers updating every 4 seconds is ~1.75M writes per second. Every write touches an indexed coordinate column, so the B-tree is being rebuilt continuously while a quarter-million readers try to walk it. That's not a slow query; that's a category error.`,`And the usual escapes don't escape. Cache the results? The answer to 'who is near me' goes stale in seconds — the cache invalidation problem IS the problem. Brute-force it in memory? Seven million distance calculations per query times a million queries per second is on the order of 10^13 haversines per second; your CPU invoice arrives before your results do. Shard Postgres harder? You're still paying index maintenance on every position tick. The only way out is a query that never scans — which means the index has to be the answer, precomputed.`],quote:`At a million QPS you don't optimize the query — you change the shape of the question.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`SQL radius query`,`Exact, familiar, one-liner`,`B-tree churn from 7M moving writers; dies well before 1M QPS`,`Prototype only`],[`Brute-force in-memory scan`,`Dead simple`,`~10^13 distance calcs/sec at scale`,`Never`],[`Square geohash grid`,`Cheap cell lookups`,`Two neighbor distances; edge distortion near poles`,`Better, not best`],[`H3 hex grid`,`O(1) updates, k-ring reads, 15 resolutions`,`New dependency; 12 pentagon edge cases`,`1M+ QPS`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Uber — Nearby drivers`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-uber-nearby.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: why the cell has to be a hexagon`,body:[`H3's core move is disarmingly simple: cover the planet in hexagons. 122 base cells at resolution 0 — 110 hexagons plus 12 unavoidable pentagons where the icosahedron folds — then 15 resolutions drilling from continent-scale (~4.4M km² per hex) down to roughly one square meter. Any lat/lon maps to a cell ID deterministically, in microseconds, with no network round-trip. That cell ID is the index.`,`Why hexagons and not squares? A square grid gives every cell two kinds of neighbors — edge-adjacent and corner-adjacent — at different distances, so every 'within radius' query carries an asterisk. A hexagon has exactly one distance to all six neighbors, so rings of cells are actually round. And of the three shapes that tile the plane, hexagons hug a circle tightest — least boundary per area covered — which means the least distortion when you're approximating '2 km from here'.`,`Then pick resolution for the product, not the planet. Res 8 is ~0.7 km² per cell with ~460 m edges — neighborhood granularity. 'Nearby' becomes: take the rider's cell, take its k-ring, union the driver sets. Seven hash lookups for the tight ring, nineteen for two steps out. No scan, ever.`],quote:`Squares gave GIS two distance metrics and a hundred asterisks. Hexagons give you one.`,table:{headers:[`Shape`,`Neighbor distances`,`Radius search`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Square`,`Two (edge vs diagonal)`,`Ring math gets fuzzy`,`Tiles, caching, map renders`],[`Triangle`,`Varies by orientation`,`Worst of the three`,`Avoid`],[`Hexagon`,`One, all 6 neighbors equal`,`Clean k-ring`,`Geo at scale`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Uber — Nearby drivers`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-uber-nearby.html`}},{heading:`03 · The write path: two map operations, done`,body:[`Now the part that actually kills geo systems: drivers move. With the hex index, a driver who crosses a cell boundary costs you three cheap steps — compute the new cell ID (microseconds, pure math), remove them from the old cell's set, add them to the new cell's set. Two map operations. No row update, no B-tree rebalance, no vacuum, no lock contention on a hot index page. And most GPS ticks don't even cross a boundary — those are true no-ops.`,`The read path gets the same gift, because you shard by hex prefix — effectively by geography. San Francisco's cells live on the SF shard; New York's on the NY shard. A rider in Brooklyn asks a question whose entire answer — their cell plus its neighbors — lives on exactly one shard. No scatter-gather, no fan-out merge, no global index to keep warm. The map is the partition key.`],quote:`When the data is the map, the map is the partition key.`,table:{headers:[`Operation`,`SQL + B-tree`,`Hex cell sets`],rows:[[`Driver moves 100 m`,`UPDATE row + index maintenance`,`SREM old cell, SADD new cell`],[`Driver moves 10 m`,`UPDATE row + index maintenance`,`No-op (same cell)`],[`Nearby query`,`Radius scan per request`,`7–19 hash lookups`],[`Shards touched`,`Depends on the planner`,`Exactly one`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Uber — Nearby drivers`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-uber-nearby.html`}},{heading:`04 · When Times Square becomes one hex: adaptive resolution`,body:[`One wrinkle: the grid is uniform but cities are not. At 6 PM, Times Square packs thousands of drivers into a couple of res-8 cells, and suddenly one hash bucket is taking stadium-level load. The cell runs hot, its shard runs hot, and your beautifully uniform index has a Manhattan-shaped hole burned in it.`,`The fix is to give the grid permission to zoom. When a cell blows past a threshold — driver count, QPS, whichever hurts first — subdivide that area to resolution N+1. Each hex splits into seven children, so the same streets now spread their load across seven times the cells, and the parent quietly becomes a routing hint instead of a hot bucket. You're not re-sharding the planet; you're zooming in on the one part that's on fire. Keep one eye on the 12 pentagons, though — their neighborhoods don't behave like hexes, so route around them on the hot path.`],quote:`A uniform grid fails non-uniformly. Adaptive resolution is how it breathes.`,table:{headers:[`Hot signal`,`Rule of thumb`,`Action`],rows:[[`Drivers per cell`,`> ~5k`,`Subdivide to res N+1`],[`QPS per cell`,`> ~10% of shard budget`,`Split reads across children`],[`Cell p99 latency`,`> 50 ms`,`Zoom in + shed coarse polls`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Uber — Nearby drivers`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-uber-nearby.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`geo-architecture`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Hex-indexed geo service: write and read topology`,caption:`Driver ticks become cell-ID set mutations; rider polls become k-ring lookups — both land on exactly one geographic shard.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  subgraph Clients["Mobile clients"]
    D["Driver app<br/>GPS every ~4 s"]
    R["Rider app<br/>polls every ~4 s"]
  end
  subgraph Edge["Geo service"]
    ENC["H3 encoder<br/>lat,lon to cell ID"]
    RT["Query router<br/>rider cell to shard"]
  end
  subgraph Store["Cell store<br/>sharded by hex prefix"]
    S1["SF shard<br/>cell to driver set"]
    S2["NY shard<br/>cell to driver set"]
    S3["Other shards"]
  end
  D -->|"position update"| ENC
  ENC -->|"SREM old, SADD new"| S1
  ENC --> S2
  ENC --> S3
  R -->|"GET /nearby"| RT
  RT -->|"k-ring lookups"| S2
  S2 -->|"unioned driver IDs"| RT
  RT -->|"drivers list"| R`},{id:`nearby-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One driver tick and one rider poll, end to end`,caption:`The write is two set operations (or a no-op); the read is 19 hash lookups and a union. Nothing scans.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant D as Driver app
  participant G as Geo API
  participant H as H3 encoder
  participant S as NY cell shard
  participant R as Rider app
  D->>G: POST /position (lat, lon)
  G->>H: encode position
  H-->>G: cell 8828308281fffff (res 8)
  alt cell changed
    G->>S: SREM driver from old cell
    G->>S: SADD driver to new cell
  else same cell
    Note over G,S: no-op
  end
  R->>G: GET /nearby (lat, lon)
  G->>H: encode rider position
  H-->>G: rider cell (res 8)
  G->>S: k-ring 2 = 19 cell lookups
  S-->>G: unioned driver IDs
  G-->>R: 200 OK, p99 under 50 ms`}],takeaways:[`Do the load math before picking a store: 1M riders polling /4s (~250k reads/s) + 7M drivers updating /4s (~1.75M writes/s) ≈ 2M ops/sec — if it doesn't fit in RAM, no query tuning saves you.`,`Prefer hexagons over squares for radius search: one uniform neighbor distance instead of two, so cell rings stay round and the math stays honest.`,`Make 'nearby' a k-ring of hash lookups, never a scan: 7 cells ≈ 1 km, 19 cells ≈ 2 km at res 8 (~0.7 km², ~460 m edges).`,`Keep position updates O(1): crossing a cell boundary is SREM old + SADD new — two map operations, zero index rebuilds; same-cell ticks are free.`,`Shard by geography and let the grid zoom: hex-prefix sharding keeps every query on one shard; subdivide hot cells to res N+1 (7× children) before they melt.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Uber — Nearby drivers`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-uber-nearby.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`},{kind:`youtube`,label:`Hello Interview — Design Uber w/ a Ex-Meta Staff Engineer: System Design Interview breakdown`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lsKU38RKQSo`,author:`Hello Interview`,note:`Designing Uber, live.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`case-whatsapp-engineering`,pathId:`case-studies`,order:15,title:`WhatsApp — 50B msgs/day · 32 engineers`,subtitle:`WhatsApp's pre-acquisition engineering team was famously tiny. The architectural choice was famously old-school: Erlang on FreeBSD, vertically scaled, no microservices, no cloud, n`,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[],story:{headline:`The 3 AM patch that never dropped a connection`,paragraphs:[`It's 3 AM and one of the chat servers is misbehaving. This box is holding a million-plus live sessions — a million phones with an open socket, mid-conversation. The standard playbook says: drain connections, roll a restart, eat the reconnect storm as a million clients hammer back at once. The WhatsApp playbook said something else: write the fix, then hot-load it into the running BEAM. Old processes finish their work on the old code; new processes spawn on the new code. No dropped sockets. No storm. No deploy window. The users never knew there was ever a problem at all.`,`Zoom out and the numbers get absurd. Pre-acquisition WhatsApp: roughly 32 engineers, ~450M monthly actives, ~50B messages a day, on about a dozen servers. That's ~$1M of revenue per engineer. No microservices. No cloud. No JavaScript. Erlang on FreeBSD, vertically scaled on hardware they tuned by hand — down to patching the kernel's TCP stack and socket allocator themselves.`,`Here's the tension worth sitting with: every 'modern' default was absent, and the system carried half a billion people. Not because the team was superhuman — because the architecture was shaped exactly like the problem, and sized exactly to the team. That shape is what we're unpacking tonight.`],problem:`How does a 32-person team carry 450M users and 50B messages a day with no microservices, no cloud, and no build pipeline?`},tech:{stack:[`Erlang/OTP`,`BEAM VM`,`FreeBSD`,`kqueue`,`Mnesia`,`Supervisor Trees`,`Hot Code Reload`],note:`A runtime whose primitive is the user session (BEAM processes), a kernel the team could patch themselves (FreeBSD + kqueue), and a store speaking the same language (Mnesia) — the minimal moving parts that let 32 people own everything.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why the default playbook dies at chat scale`,body:[`Here's the thing about chat: it's not a compute problem, it's a connection problem. A million connections that are mostly idle, waiting. Run thread-per-connection on a JVM and each thread drags roughly a megabyte of stack — that's a terabyte of memory before you've routed a single message. So you reach for the async event-loop pattern, and it works... except now every session is a hand-rolled state machine, and every feature is more callback plumbing.`,`And why not microservices? Do the headcount math. With 32 engineers, every service boundary is a person's job — a person on-call, a person writing the client, a person owning the deploy. Microservices also put extra network hops on a latency-sensitive path where 'message delivered' is the whole product. WhatsApp's answer was one fat, deeply-tuned binary per role, and nothing else.`,`So the constraint set falls out cleanly: keep connections cheap, keep the delivery path short, keep the ops surface small enough for one team to hold in their heads. Every choice downstream is just those three rules applied again.`],quote:`Chat is a connection problem wearing a compute costume — budget memory per connection, not per request.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Thread per connection`,`Dead-simple code`,`~1MB stack each; 1M conns ≈ 1TB RAM`,`Dead on arrival`],[`Async event loop (C10K)`,`Low memory footprint`,`Hand-rolled state machines, callback sprawl`,`Workable, expensive to maintain`],[`Erlang process per session`,`Few KB each, isolated GC, mailbox = message`,`Exotic language, hiring risk`,`Near-perfect fit`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — WhatsApp — 50B msgs/day · 32 engineers`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-whatsapp-engineering.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: one user, one process`,body:[`This is where Erlang stops looking quirky and starts looking inevitable. A user's session is one lightweight BEAM process — a few KB of stack, its own heap, its own garbage collector. No stop-the-world pauses, ever, because there's no shared heap to freeze. And a message? It's literally an Erlang message dropped into a mailbox. The domain and the runtime speak the same language, so the glue code WhatsApp needed was almost nothing. They started from an ejabberd fork and reshaped it, rather than fighting a framework.`,`Now the failure mode. Why not wrap everything in try/rescue and handle errors defensively? Because defensive code hides bugs behind half-clean state. Erlang's supervisor trees invert that: let it crash. A broken session dies, the supervisor restarts it in microseconds with pristine state, and the client just reconnects. Failure becomes a restart policy, not a code path you have to audit.`,`And the shape at scale: roughly one chat-server node per million active users, with Rick Reed reporting 2M+ concurrent TCP connections on a single node. Routing a message is a mailbox send between processes — same node or across nodes. That's the entire delivery mechanism. No broker, no sidecar, no service mesh.`],quote:`You can build Erlang in any language — but you'll only do it once.`,source:{label:`sharpoverride — WhatsApp — 50B msgs/day · 32 engineers`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-whatsapp-engineering.html`}},{heading:`03 · Tuning the metal: FreeBSD, kqueue, and a hand-patched kernel`,body:[`Why FreeBSD in 2013? Two reasons. First, kqueue and the network stack were better tuned for this exact workload than Linux's epoll at the time. Second — and this is the underrated part — owning the kernel meant they could patch it. TCP stack tweaks, a custom socket allocator, scheduler changes. When your bottleneck lives below the OS, having the source and the skill is the whole game.`,`Now the economics, because this is the part the cloud era forgot. One carefully-tuned machine holding a million-plus connections is often cheaper than a hundred un-tuned instances each holding ten thousand. Why not just autoscale? Because chat load is steady and predictable — you know your daily curve to the digit. A dozen boxes you know intimately beat a hundred you provisioned by dashboard.`,`The lesson is not 'install FreeBSD.' The lesson is that vertical scale on tuned hardware is a legitimate, neglected strategy — and that per-instance overhead compounds silently until your fleet is mostly babysitting itself.`],quote:`The cost of one giant, carefully-tuned machine is often less than a fleet of un-tuned ones.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Horizontal cloud fleet`,`Elastic, familiar, vendor-backed`,`Ops surface, noisy neighbors, per-instance overhead`,`Spiky, heterogeneous workloads`],[`Vertical tuned boxes`,`Deep per-box performance, tiny ops surface`,`Hardware lead time, single-box blast radius`,`Steady, predictable, connection-heavy loads`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — WhatsApp — 50B msgs/day · 32 engineers`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-whatsapp-engineering.html`}},{heading:`04 · The culture of constraint: owning every layer`,body:[`The tooling list reads like a prank: no JIRA, no standups, no architecture committee, no build pipeline. Code was hot-loaded into production BEAM nodes — that 3 AM story from the top isn't an exception, it was the deploy process. That sounds reckless until you see the precondition: it's only safe if you own every layer and the system is uniform enough to reason about as one thing.`,`And that's the honest caveat. This shape fits chat because chat is uniform — one workload, one delivery path, one latency target. Feeds, ads, and ML pipelines are heterogeneous; they genuinely need more moving parts and more team structure. WhatsApp's lesson isn't 'do this.' It's that architecture is a headcount decision: the number of moving parts you can operate equals the number of people you have.`,`So when you're tempted to add a service, a queue, or a vendor — ask what layer you just gave away the ability to fix at 3 AM. Constraint wasn't their limitation. It was their leverage.`],quote:`Every layer you don't own is a layer you can't fix at 3 AM.`,source:{label:`sharpoverride — WhatsApp — 50B msgs/day · 32 engineers`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-whatsapp-engineering.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`whatsapp-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`WhatsApp chat topology — a dozen tuned nodes, not a fleet`,caption:`Clients terminate on the connection edge; each chat node holds ~1M+ BEAM session processes. Delivery is a mailbox send; offline messages queue in Mnesia and fire a push.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
  A["Mobile clients<br/>iOS / Android"] --> EDGE["Connection edge<br/>TCP / TLS"]
  EDGE --> C1["Chat server node 1<br/>~1M+ sessions"]
  EDGE --> C2["Chat server node 2<br/>~1M+ sessions"]
  EDGE --> C3["Chat server node N<br/>~12 servers total"]
  C1 -->|"mailbox send"| RTR["Message routing<br/>process-to-process"]
  C2 -->|"mailbox send"| RTR
  C3 -->|"mailbox send"| RTR
  RTR -->|"recipient online"| C2
  RTR -->|"recipient offline"| Q["Offline message queue<br/>Mnesia"]
  Q --> P["Push gateways<br/>APNs / GCM"]
  Q -->|"drain on reconnect"| C2`},{id:`message-delivery-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One message, two paths`,caption:`Online delivery is a mailbox send and a receipt. Offline delivery persists to Mnesia, pushes a notification, and drains on reconnect — no message is ever lost in between.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  participant S as Sender device
  participant CS as Chat server (BEAM)
  participant RP as Recipient session process
  participant Q as Offline queue (Mnesia)
  participant P as APNs / GCM
  S->>CS: SEND message (encrypted payload)
  CS->>RP: mailbox message
  alt recipient online
    RP->>CS: deliver to recipient device
    CS-->>S: delivered receipt
  else recipient offline
    CS->>Q: persist message
    Q->>P: push notification
    Note over RP: device reconnects later
    RP->>Q: drain queue
    Q-->>RP: missed messages
  end`}],takeaways:[`One user = one process with a few-KB stack: if your per-connection memory is measured in MB, a million connections costs a terabyte — pick a runtime whose concurrency unit matches the domain.`,`Run the users-per-engineer number: WhatsApp did ~14M MAU per engineer (~$1M MRR each). Every service boundary costs headcount — spend architectural complexity like payroll.`,`Measure cost per million connections, not per instance: one hand-tuned FreeBSD box held 1M+ (later 2M+) concurrent TCP connections — vertical tuning can beat a default cloud fleet.`,`Let it crash: supervisor trees restart a dead session in microseconds with clean state — restart policy is cheaper and safer than defensive exception-handling code.`,`Hot code reload buys zero-downtime patches but demands total ownership of kernel, VM, and app — only take that deal if you can fix every layer at 3 AM.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — WhatsApp — 50B msgs/day · 32 engineers`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-whatsapp-engineering.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`case-zoom`,pathId:`case-studies`,order:16,title:`Zoom — 300M video calls/day`,subtitle:`March 2020. Zoom's daily participant count went from ~10M to ~300M in 90 days. The infrastructure didn't melt. The story is half engineering investment they'd been making and half `,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[],story:{headline:`The graph went vertical. The architecture didn't flinch.`,paragraphs:[`March 2020, and the dashboard looks broken. The daily-participant line — the one that took Zoom years to push to 10 million — doubles in two weeks, then keeps climbing. Schools close, offices close, the whole world pivots to video, and by April the number is 300 million. That's 30x in 90 days, on a product where every user is uploading live video and downloading everyone else's, in real time, with no cache to hide behind.`,`The war room that month was strange: not panic, procurement. Because the scaling story was boring by design. A meeting maps to an SFU node; an SFU node forwards packets and never touches pixels; so capacity is literally a count of nodes. The team added thousands of servers in weeks — including a famous emergency deal with Oracle Cloud — pointed the fabric at them, and the software didn't change.`,`The counterfactual is what keeps capacity engineers up at night. If Zoom had built an MCU — server-side mixing — every new participant would have burned transcoding CPU, every layout change would have forced a re-encode, and 30x growth would have meant 30x CPU farms plus a redesign mid-pandemic. Instead the client composes the grid, the server just moves bytes, and an architecture built years earlier absorbed the spike.`],problem:`How do you absorb a 30x demand spike in 90 days on a product whose core cost is real-time video bandwidth — without re-architecting anything?`},tech:{stack:[`SFU Forwarding`,`Simulcast Layers`,`UDP Transport`,`Client Composition`,`Multi-DC Fabric`],note:`An SFU relays pre-encoded streams so servers pay for bandwidth instead of transcoding CPU; simulcast gives each sender multiple quality layers for the SFU to pick from; and a multi-datacenter fabric turns capacity growth into 'add more nodes.'`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why a 30x spike usually kills you`,body:[`Here's the number that should terrify anyone doing capacity planning: 10 million daily participants to 300 million in about 90 days. That's 30x. Most systems fall over at 2x, and we run war games for 5x. And video is the worst case — every participant is simultaneously an uploader and N-1 downloads, in real time, with no batching and no caching. There is no 'eventually consistent' in a live call.`,`So when the graph went vertical, the question on the war room floor wasn't 'how many servers do we buy' — it was the scarier one: 'does buying servers actually buy capacity?' For CPU-bound, stateful workloads it doesn't; you just get more expensive melting. For Zoom it did, and that's the whole story. The unit of work — forward one stream — is independent per node. Meetings map cleanly onto SFU nodes. Capacity is embarrassingly parallel.`],quote:`Scaling isn't adding servers — it's whether adding servers adds capacity.`,table:{headers:[`Growth shock`,`What usually breaks`,`Why Zoom held`],rows:[[`2x`,`Connection pools, cache hit rates`,`More nodes, same software`],[`10x`,`Hot shards, stateful bottlenecks`,`Meetings map cleanly to SFU nodes`],[`30x in 90 days`,`The architecture itself`,`Forwarding is linear: 30x load ≈ 30x nodes`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Zoom — 300M video calls/day`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-zoom.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: SFU vs MCU vs P2P`,body:[`Let's walk the boundary. There were two mainstream ways to build multi-party video, and the choice was made years before the pandemic. MCU: the server decodes every incoming stream, mixes them into one composite, re-encodes, and ships a single output per participant. Great for the client's bandwidth, brutal for the server — and every layout change, gallery to speaker view, forces a re-encode of everything.`,`SFU flips it: the server is a smart router. It never touches pixels — it forwards pre-encoded streams, and the client does the composition, drawing the grid locally. Why not a P2P mesh? For a 1:1 call it's beautiful, but at N participants every client is pushing N-1 copies up a home uplink, and you lose the central point for recording and quality control. So: SFU. Server cost per participant is bandwidth, not CPU — one node carries hundreds of meetings.`],quote:`Push the pixels to the edge. The server's job is bandwidth, not CPU.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`MCU mixing`,`One stream per client, cheap uplink`,`Server transcoding CPU; re-encode on every layout change`,`Small calls, legacy interop`],[`SFU forwarding`,`Server just relays; hundreds of meetings per node`,`Clients download N-1 streams`,`Large calls, massive scale`],[`P2P mesh`,`No servers at all`,`N² uploads from every client's uplink`,`1:1 and tiny groups`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Zoom — 300M video calls/day`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-zoom.html`}},{heading:`03 · The O(N²) tax — and how simulcast pays it down`,body:[`Now the honest math. N participants, each uploads one stream, the SFU forwards N-1 copies — that's O(N²). A 100-person call is roughly 10,000 stream copies flowing through SFUs. That's the tax, and you don't eliminate it — you shrink it.`,`Simulcast is how. Each sender encodes their camera once at three qualities — 180p, 360p, 720p — at the edge, upfront. The SFU never transcodes; it just picks which pre-encoded layer each recipient gets. Phone on 4G? 180p. One tile among thirty in gallery view? 180p. Active speaker in full-screen? 720p. The recipient's client signals its available downlink and the SFU switches layers in real time. The O(N²) stays, but the payloads for most recipients collapse.`],quote:`Encode once at the edge, forward many in the middle, decode everywhere.`,table:{headers:[`Recipient`,`Layer forwarded`,`Why`],rows:[[`Active speaker view`,`720p`,`You're staring at one face — spend bits there`],[`Gallery tile`,`180p`,`Thirty tiny thumbnails don't need resolution`],[`Phone on 4G`,`180p`,`Downlink budget is the constraint`],[`Screen share viewer`,`High-res content stream`,`Text needs bits to stay legible`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Zoom — 300M video calls/day`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-zoom.html`}},{heading:`04 · The boring superpower: capacity you built years ago`,body:[`Here's the part I want you to sit with: none of this was built in 2020. The SFU decision, the simulcast, the multi-datacenter fabric — years of investment that looked like over-engineering right up until March. When the spike hit, the playbook was almost boring: add SFU nodes, meetings get assigned, capacity goes up linearly. Thousands of servers arrived in weeks, and the software didn't change.`,`That's the difference between capacity as a build decision and a deploy decision. Autoscaling adjusts instance counts; it cannot fix an architecture whose per-request cost is CPU-heavy stateful work. If your scaling story is 'spin up more of the same thing and it gets proportionally bigger,' you can ride a 30x spike. If it isn't, no dashboard alarm will save you.`],quote:`You can't auto-scale your way out of 300 million users. You have to have already built the architecture.`,table:{headers:[`Decision`,`When it was made`,`What it bought in 2020`],rows:[[`SFU over MCU`,`Years earlier`,`Server cost = bandwidth, not transcoding CPU`],[`Simulcast layers`,`Years earlier`,`Bandwidth per recipient bounded and adaptive`],[`Multi-DC fabric`,`Years earlier`,`New nodes absorb the spike, same software`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Zoom — 300M video calls/day`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-zoom.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`sfu-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One meeting, one SFU: forward, never transcode`,caption:`Each sender uploads one simulcast set; the SFU forwards a per-recipient layer. Server cost is bandwidth, not CPU.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  subgraph Senders
    A["Participant A<br/>encodes 180p / 360p / 720p"]
    B["Participant B<br/>encodes 180p / 360p / 720p"]
    C["Participant C<br/>encodes 180p / 360p / 720p"]
  end
  SFU["SFU node<br/>forward only - no transcoding"]
  subgraph Receivers
    D["Phone on 4G<br/>gets 180p"]
    E["Desktop gallery<br/>gets 180p tiles"]
    F["Desktop speaker view<br/>gets 720p"]
  end
  A -->|"1 uplink stream"| SFU
  B -->|"1 uplink stream"| SFU
  C -->|"1 uplink stream"| SFU
  SFU -->|"selected layer per recipient"| D
  SFU -->|"selected layer per recipient"| E
  SFU -->|"selected layer per recipient"| F`},{id:`layer-adaptation`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Adaptive layer selection in real time`,caption:`The client signals its downlink; the SFU switches between pre-encoded layers with zero transcoding on the path.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  participant E as Sender
  participant S as SFU
  participant C as Recipient on 4G
  E->>S: Uploads 180p + 360p + 720p once
  C->>S: Joins, reports downlink ~2 Mbps
  S->>C: Forwards 180p layer
  C->>S: Network improves, more headroom
  S->>C: Switches to 360p, then 720p
  Note over E,C: No transcoding anywhere - the SFU only picks which pre-encoded layer to relay`}],takeaways:[`Capacity is a build decision, not a deploy decision — Zoom's 30x spike was absorbed by architecture built years earlier, not by emergency redesign.`,`In an SFU, server cost per participant is forwarding bandwidth, not transcoding CPU — a single node carries hundreds of meetings.`,`A meeting of N participants costs O(N²) stream copies (~10,000 for 100 people); simulcast layers (180p/360p/720p) keep most of those copies tiny.`,`Encode once at the sender, forward in the middle, compose at the client — never put transcoding on the hot path.`,`If adding nodes doesn't add capacity, autoscaling won't save you — verify that 2x servers = 2x load before the spike, not during it.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Zoom — 300M video calls/day`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/case-zoom.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`}];export{e as lessons};